INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','easy','flashcard','What is the first-line antihypertensive for uncomplicated hypertension in a Black patient per Ghana STG 2017?','["First-line agents in Black patients are thiazide diuretics (bendroflumethiazide 2.5mg OD or hydrochlorothiazide 12.5-25mg OD) OR calcium channel blockers (amlodipine 5-10mg OD or nifedipine retard 10-40mg BD). ACE inhibitors and ARBs are NOT first-line in Black patients because lower plasma renin activity reduces their efficacy. CCBs and thiazides target the salt-sensitive, low-renin hypertension predominant in this population. If target BP is not achieved, combine both agents before adding a second-line drug such as methyldopa or hydralazine."]','A','Mnemonic: A-B-C-D — Avoid ACE inhibitor and Beta-blocker monotherapy as FIRST LINE in Black patients; Choose CCB or Diuretic first.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','easy','flashcard','Define hypertensive emergency and give the target BP reduction in the first hour.','["Hypertensive emergency is severely elevated BP (usually >180/120 mmHg) with acute end-organ damage — hypertensive encephalopathy, AKI, aortic dissection, acute pulmonary oedema, or MACE. Target: reduce mean arterial pressure (MAP) by no more than 20-25% in the first hour to avoid ischaemia from loss of autoregulation. Ghana STG first-line IV agents: labetalol 20-50mg IV bolus or hydralazine 5-10mg IV. Sublingual nifedipine is CONTRAINDICATED due to unpredictable precipitous BP fall causing stroke or MI."]','A','Never use sublingual nifedipine in hypertensive emergency — it causes uncontrolled BP drop and can precipitate MI or stroke.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','easy','flashcard','What BP level defines Stage 2 hypertension and what end-organ damage signs should you look for?','["Stage 2 hypertension: SBP ≥160 mmHg OR DBP ≥100 mmHg (JNC 8/Ghana STG classification). End-organ damage signs: EYES — hypertensive retinopathy (AV nipping, flame haemorrhages, papilloedema in grade IV); BRAIN — headache, stroke, TIA, encephalopathy; HEART — LVH (S4 gallop, ECG voltage criteria), heart failure; KIDNEYS — proteinuria, raised creatinine (hypertensive nephropathy); VESSELS — aortic aneurysm, peripheral arterial disease. Always do urinalysis, ECG, and fundoscopy at diagnosis."]','A','Grade III retinopathy (haemorrhages + exudates) vs Grade IV (papilloedema) — only Grade IV constitutes hypertensive emergency retinopathy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','medium','flashcard','How does white coat hypertension differ from masked hypertension, and how is each confirmed?','["White coat hypertension: BP elevated in clinic (≥140/90) but normal on ambulatory BP monitoring (ABPM) — average daytime ABPM <135/85 mmHg. Masked hypertension: BP normal in clinic but elevated on ABPM — higher cardiovascular risk than white coat. Both are confirmed by ABPM (gold standard) or home BP monitoring. ABPM is also indicated for: suspected hypertension (clinic readings 140/90-179/110), assessing nocturnal dipping (non-dippers have worse prognosis), evaluating treatment response, and hypotensive episodes. Normal 24h ABPM: <130/80 mmHg; daytime <135/85; night-time <120/70."]','A','Non-dippers (nocturnal BP fall <10%) have increased risk of LVH, CKD, and cardiovascular events — always check dipping status on ABPM.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','medium','flashcard','What clinical clues suggest renovascular hypertension, and what is the investigation of choice?','["Renovascular hypertension clues: resistant hypertension, sudden-onset or worsening hypertension in young woman (fibromuscular dysplasia) or older atherosclerotic male, epigastric/flank bruit, flash pulmonary oedema, BP asymmetry between arms, >30% rise in creatinine after starting ACE inhibitor (bilateral RAS), hypokalaemia (secondary hyperaldosteronism). Investigation of choice: CT angiography or MR angiography of renal arteries; Doppler ultrasound is a non-invasive screen. Definitive treatment: renal artery angioplasty ± stenting (FMD) or medical management (atherosclerotic RAS). ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in bilateral renal artery stenosis."]','A','The ACE inhibitor challenge (creatinine rise >30%) is a bedside clue for bilateral RAS — always check creatinine 1-2 weeks after starting ACE-I.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','medium','flashcard','Define resistant hypertension and list its common causes.','["Resistant hypertension: BP remains above target despite 3 antihypertensive drugs of different classes at optimal doses (one MUST be a diuretic). Pseudo-resistance must be excluded first — poor adherence, white coat effect, inadequate dose, drug interactions. True causes: SHAPE mnemonic — Sleep apnoea (OSA), Hyperaldosteronism (primary), Aortic coarctation/renal Artery stenosis, Phaeochromocytoma, Endocrine (Cushing''s, hypothyroidism). Investigation: aldosterone:renin ratio (primary hyperaldosteronism), 24h urinary catecholamines (phaeochromocytoma), overnight dexamethasone suppression test (Cushing''s). Add spironolactone 25mg as the 4th agent — most effective add-on in resistant HBP."]','A','Spironolactone is the most effective 4th agent in resistant hypertension — trials show ~20 mmHg SBP reduction, especially if aldosterone excess is driving resistance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','medium','flashcard','What are the BP targets in hypertension with diabetes mellitus and with chronic kidney disease?','["Diabetes mellitus: BP target <130/80 mmHg (ADA/ESC 2018); first-line agent is ACE inhibitor or ARB (renoprotective, reduces albuminuria). However, per Ghana STG — in Black diabetics, still consider CCB or thiazide alongside ACE-I given lower ACE-I efficacy as monotherapy. CKD (proteinuria ≥300mg/day): BP target <130/80 mmHg; ACE inhibitor or ARB is first-line (reduces intraglomerular pressure, slows CKD progression). Never combine ACE-I + ARB (dual RAAS blockade) — increases risk of hyperkalaemia, AKI, and hypotension (ONTARGET trial). Monitor eGFR and potassium after starting RAAS blockers."]','A','DUAL RAAS BLOCKADE (ACE-I + ARB together) is PROHIBITED — the ONTARGET trial showed increased harm with no additional benefit.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','hard','flashcard','A 34-year-old woman with hypertension develops hypokalaemia and hypertension worsening on ACE inhibitor. What is the likely diagnosis and diagnostic approach?','["Likely diagnosis: Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn''s syndrome) — autonomous aldosterone production causing sodium retention, hypertension, and hypokalaemia (not always present). Diagnostic approach: 1) Stop interfering drugs (aldosterone antagonists, beta-blockers, ACE-I for 2-6 weeks). 2) Measure plasma aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR) — ratio >30 (with aldosterone >15 ng/dL) is positive screen. 3) Confirmatory test: salt loading or fludrocortisone suppression test. 4) Adrenal CT scan — but imaging can miss microadenomas. 5) Adrenal vein sampling (gold standard) to lateralise before surgery. Treatment: unilateral adenoma → laparoscopic adrenalectomy; bilateral hyperplasia → spironolactone/eplerenone."]','A','Hypokalaemia is only present in ~30-50% of primary hyperaldosteronism cases — do NOT use normokalaemia to exclude the diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','hard','flashcard','A hypertensive patient presents with headache, sweating, palpitations, and BP 210/120 mmHg. Phaeochromocytoma is suspected. What investigations confirm it and what is the danger in prescribing a beta-blocker alone?','["Phaeochromocytoma investigations: First-line — 24-hour urinary metanephrines and catecholamines OR plasma free metanephrines (most sensitive, >97%). Imaging: adrenal CT or MRI after biochemical confirmation; MIBG scintigraphy for extra-adrenal or metastatic disease. DANGER of beta-blocker alone: causes unopposed alpha-adrenergic stimulation → paradoxical severe hypertension and hypertensive crisis (peripheral vasoconstriction without beta-vasodilatory opposition). ALWAYS give alpha-blocker FIRST (phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin) for ≥14 days before adding beta-blocker. Pre-operative volume expansion with IV saline is also essential to prevent rebound hypotension post-adrenalectomy."]','A','Rule of 10s for phaeochromocytoma: 10% bilateral, 10% extra-adrenal, 10% malignant, 10% in children, 10% familial (MEN2, VHL, NF1).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Hypertension','hard','flashcard','Describe the management of hypertension in pregnancy, including which drugs are safe and which are contraindicated.','["Safe antihypertensives in pregnancy: Methyldopa (first-line, longest safety record), labetalol (IV for acute, oral for chronic), nifedipine (oral, tocolytic concern in 1st trimester but generally used), hydralazine (IV for emergency). CONTRAINDICATED: ACE inhibitors (fetal renal dysgenesis, oligohydramnios, death — contraindicated in 2nd and 3rd trimesters), ARBs (same as ACE-I), thiazide diuretics (reduce placental perfusion), atenolol (IUGR). Gestational hypertension: BP ≥140/90 after 20 weeks without proteinuria. Pre-eclampsia: hypertension + proteinuria ± features (headache, visual disturbance, RUQ pain, thrombocytopaenia, raised LFTs). Eclampsia treatment: IV magnesium sulphate (anticonvulsant), not diazepam; deliver if ≥37 weeks or if haemodynamically unstable."]','A','Magnesium sulphate is the anticonvulsant of choice in eclampsia — it also prevents recurrent seizures. Check: Respiratory rate >12, Urine output >25mL/h, Patellar reflex present (toxicity monitor).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','easy','flashcard','What ECG criteria define a STEMI and what is the door-to-balloon time target?','["STEMI ECG criteria: new ST elevation at J-point in ≥2 contiguous leads — ≥1mm in limb leads or ≥2mm in V1-V4 (≥2.5mm in men <40 years in V2-V3). New LBBB with ischaemic symptoms is treated as STEMI equivalent. Door-to-balloon (primary PCI) target: <90 minutes from first medical contact. If PCI not available within 120 minutes, give thrombolysis within 12 hours of symptom onset. Anatomical territories: inferior STEMI (II, III, aVF) = RCA; anterior STEMI (V1-V4) = LAD; lateral STEMI (I, aVL, V5-V6) = circumflex. Always get a right-sided ECG (V4R) in inferior STEMI to detect RV infarction — contraindicate nitrates if RV MI present."]','A','Right-sided ECG in ALL inferior STEMIs — RV infarction is diagnosed by ST elevation in V4R and is a contraindication to nitrates (will cause severe hypotension).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','easy','flashcard','How do you distinguish NSTEMI from unstable angina, and what is the initial treatment bundle per Ghana STG?','["NSTEMI vs unstable angina: Both present with ischaemic chest pain at rest or minimal exertion WITHOUT persistent ST elevation. Key difference: NSTEMI has elevated cardiac troponin (above 99th percentile of normal reference population); unstable angina has normal troponin. Troponin peaks at 12-24h; repeat at 0h and 3h (high-sensitivity) or 0h and 6h (conventional). Ghana STG initial ACS bundle (MONAC): Morphine IV 2-4mg for pain, Oxygen (if SpO2 <94%), Nitrates (GTN sublingual 0.4mg every 5 min x3), Aspirin 300mg loading dose, Clopidogrel 300mg loading dose + Enoxaparin SC 1mg/kg BD (anticoagulation). Avoid NSAIDS."]','A','Mnemonic MONAC: Morphine, Oxygen, Nitrates, Aspirin, Clopidogrel — the initial ACS treatment bundle. Enoxaparin is the anticoagulant of choice in Ghana STG.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','easy','flashcard','What medications should every post-MI patient be discharged on (secondary prevention)?','["Post-MI secondary prevention — 4 pillars (ABS + Statin): 1) Aspirin 75-100mg OD lifelong (antiplatelet, reduces re-infarction). 2) Beta-blocker (bisoprolol/carvedilol/metoprolol) — reduces mortality, prevents arrhythmias, reduces LV remodelling; continue for minimum 1-3 years (lifelong if LV dysfunction). 3) ACE inhibitor (lisinopril/ramipril) — reduces mortality, prevents LV remodelling; lifelong if EF reduced. 4) Statin (atorvastatin 40-80mg) — high-intensity statin regardless of baseline LDL; reduces MACE. Add dual antiplatelet (clopidogrel/ticagrelor/prasugrel) for 12 months post-STEMI/NSTEMI. Aldosterone antagonist (eplerenone) if EF <40% + HF or diabetes post-MI."]','A','The 4 pillars post-MI: A (Aspirin + second antiplatelet), B (Beta-blocker), C (CCB only if needed), S (Statin + ACE-I/ARB). Lifelong therapy for all 4.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','medium','flashcard','What are the Killip classification stages for acute MI and their associated mortality?','["Killip classification grades acute MI severity by clinical signs of heart failure: Class I — no heart failure (no crackles, no S3); mortality ~6%. Class II — mild HF (bibasal crackles <50% lung fields, S3 gallop, elevated JVP); mortality ~17%. Class III — acute pulmonary oedema (crackles >50% lung fields); mortality ~38%. Class IV — cardiogenic shock (SBP <90 mmHg, signs of hypoperfusion: cold clammy, oliguria, altered consciousness); mortality ~67-81%. Higher Killip class at presentation predicts worse in-hospital prognosis. Cardiogenic shock (Killip IV) is managed with dobutamine infusion, IABP, and emergency PCI."]','A','Killip class is assessed AT PRESENTATION — it is the most important clinical predictor of 30-day mortality in acute MI and guides ICU admission decisions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','medium','flashcard','What is Dressler''s syndrome, when does it occur, and how is it treated?','["Dressler''s syndrome (post-MI pericarditis): autoimmune pericarditis occurring 2-10 weeks after MI (or cardiac surgery — post-pericardiotomy syndrome). Mechanism: immune response to released cardiac antigens. Features: fever, pleuritic chest pain, pericardial friction rub, pleural effusion, raised ESR/CRP, new ST elevation (diffuse, saddle-shaped). Distinguish from early post-MI pericarditis (epistenocardiac pericarditis) which occurs within 72 hours directly over the infarcted area. Treatment: aspirin (1-2g TDS) + colchicine 0.5mg BD for 3 months; avoid NSAIDs in first 4 weeks post-MI (impair myocardial healing); steroids only if refractory. Anticoagulation should be discontinued if large effusion present."]','A','Dressler''s syndrome vs epistenocardiac pericarditis: Dressler = weeks later, systemic features, autoimmune; Epistenocardiac = within 72h, localised, directly over infarct.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','medium','flashcard','Name the mechanical complications of acute MI, their timing, and how they present.','["Mechanical complications of MI — timing and presentation: 1) Free wall rupture — days 3-7 post-MI; sudden haemodynamic collapse, cardiac tamponade, pulseless electrical activity (PEA); near-universally fatal without emergency surgery. 2) Ventricular septal defect (VSD) — days 3-7; new harsh holosystolic murmur at left sternal edge + biventricular failure; confirmed by echo (step-up in oxygen saturation from RA to PA). 3) Papillary muscle rupture → acute MR — days 3-7 post-inferior MI (posteromedial papillary supplied by single vessel); sudden acute pulmonary oedema + new systolic murmur; echo confirms flail leaflet. All require emergency surgical repair. All occur in the subacute softening phase of myocardial necrosis."]','A','All 3 mechanical complications share the same peak timing (3-7 days post-MI) and all present with sudden haemodynamic deterioration — echo is the bedside differentiator.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','medium','flashcard','What are the absolute contraindications to thrombolysis in STEMI?','["Absolute contraindications to thrombolysis: PATCH mnemonic — Prior haemorrhagic stroke (ever) or ischaemic stroke within 3 months; Active internal bleeding (not menstruation); Trauma/surgery/head injury within 3 weeks; Confirmed aortic dissection; Hypertension uncontrolled (SBP >180 or DBP >110 at time of lysis); also intracranial neoplasm/AVM, suspected aortic dissection, and known coagulopathy. Relative contraindications: BP 160-180/100-110, prolonged CPR >10min, pregnancy, anticoagulant therapy, prior streptokinase use (use alteplase instead). Ghana STG uses streptokinase 1.5 million units IV over 60 min as thrombolytic of choice where PCI unavailable."]','A','Streptokinase is antigenic — do NOT re-use within 12 months (antibodies cause allergic reaction and reduced efficacy). Use alteplase for repeat thrombolysis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','hard','flashcard','Explain the no-reflow phenomenon in primary PCI and the TIMI flow grading system.','["No-reflow phenomenon: restoration of epicardial coronary patency after PCI but failure of myocardial reperfusion at microvascular level. Causes: microvascular spasm, distal embolisation of thrombus/plaque debris, ischaemia-reperfusion injury, microvascular oedema. ECG evidence: persistent ST elevation despite open artery on angiogram; poor STR (ST resolution <50%). Treatment: intracoronary adenosine, verapamil, or GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors; aspiration thrombectomy may help. TIMI Flow Grades: 0 = no flow beyond occlusion; 1 = minimal penetration of contrast; 2 = partial perfusion (slow); 3 = normal perfusion. TIMI 3 flow post-PCI is the goal — strongly correlates with 30-day mortality reduction."]','A','TIMI 3 flow is the procedural target — TIMI 2 flow (partial) post-PCI is associated with similar mortality to TIMI 0/1, highlighting the all-or-none nature of reperfusion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','hard','flashcard','How is the TIMI Risk Score calculated for NSTEMI/unstable angina and what score warrants early invasive strategy?','["TIMI Risk Score (NSTEMI/UA) — 7 variables, 1 point each: 1) Age ≥65 years. 2) ≥3 CAD risk factors (family hx, HTN, hyperlipidaemia, DM, smoking). 3) Prior coronary stenosis ≥50%. 4) ST deviation on presenting ECG. 5) ≥2 anginal events in prior 24 hours. 6) Use of aspirin in prior 7 days (suggests aspirin-resistant disease). 7) Elevated serum cardiac markers (troponin/CK-MB). Score 0-2: low risk (4.7% event rate) → can manage conservatively. Score 3-4: intermediate risk → consider early invasive strategy within 24-72h. Score 5-7: high risk (41% event rate) → urgent invasive strategy within 24 hours. Score ≥3 generally warrants GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor or early angiography."]','A','Aspirin use in prior 7 days INCREASES the TIMI score — it identifies patients who developed ACS despite aspirin therapy, indicating higher-risk, more thrombogenic plaques.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Acute Coronary Syndromes','hard','flashcard','A 58-year-old diabetic with STEMI develops cardiogenic shock 6 hours after onset. Describe the haemodynamic profile and management.','["Cardiogenic shock haemodynamics: Low CO (<2.2 L/min/m²), high PCWP (>18 mmHg), high SVR (compensatory vasoconstriction) — the classic ''cold and wet'' profile. Clinical: SBP <90 mmHg, HR >100, cold clammy peripheries, oliguria <30mL/h, altered consciousness. Management: 1) Emergency PCI (primary reperfusion even in shock — SHOCK trial: 30-day mortality benefit). 2) Vasopressors: dobutamine (inotrope, reduces afterload — first choice) ± noradrenaline (if profound hypotension, vasoplegic component). 3) Intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP): reduces afterload, augments diastolic pressure; Ghana STG recommends dobutamine as pharmacological bridge. 4) Treat precipitants (arrhythmia, mechanical complication). 5) Consider Impella/LVAD if refractory."]','A','SHOCK trial: early revascularisation (PCI/CABG) reduces 6-month mortality in cardiogenic shock even when initiated up to 54 hours after MI onset — do not delay.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','easy','flashcard','What are the Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS) classes of stable angina?','["CCS Classification: Class I — angina only with strenuous/rapid/prolonged exertion; ordinary activity does not cause angina. Class II — slight limitation of ordinary activity; angina on walking >2 blocks on level ground or climbing >1 flight of stairs at normal pace. Class III — marked limitation of ordinary physical activity; angina on walking 1-2 blocks or climbing 1 flight at normal pace. Class IV — inability to carry on ANY physical activity without discomfort; angina may be present at rest. CCS class guides treatment escalation: Class I-II → medical therapy; Class III-IV or refractory → consider revascularisation (PCI or CABG). Document CCS class at each visit to assess treatment response."]','A','CCS is the ANGINA equivalent of NYHA for heart failure. CCS III-IV = significant functional impairment requiring escalation beyond single-agent therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','easy','flashcard','What are the three features of TYPICAL angina and how does GTN work?','["Typical angina requires ALL 3: 1) Substernal chest discomfort with a characteristic quality/duration (pressure, squeezing, heaviness lasting 5-15 min). 2) Provoked by exertion or emotional stress. 3) Relieved by rest or GTN within 5 minutes. Atypical angina = 2 of 3. Non-cardiac chest pain = 0-1 of 3. GTN mechanism: nitric oxide donor → smooth muscle relaxation → venodilation (preload reduction, main effect) + coronary arterial dilation + modest arteriolar dilation (afterload reduction). Use: GTN 0.4mg sublingual, sit down before use to avoid syncope, repeat every 5 minutes up to 3 doses; if pain persists after 3 doses — call emergency services (may be ACS). Store GTN away from light and heat; expires 8 weeks after opening."]','A','GTN causes postural hypotension — always instruct patients to SIT DOWN before using. If standing, they may faint due to sudden preload reduction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','easy','flashcard','When is CABG preferred over PCI in stable CAD?','["CABG (coronary artery bypass grafting) is preferred over PCI when: 1) Left main coronary artery disease (≥50% stenosis) — CABG reduces mortality vs PCI (SYNTAX trial). 2) Triple-vessel disease with diabetes mellitus — CABG superior (FREEDOM trial). 3) Triple-vessel disease with complex lesions (SYNTAX score >33). 4) Failed PCI or anatomy not suitable for PCI. 5) Associated valve disease requiring surgery. 6) Chronic total occlusion with poor collaterals. LIMA (left internal mammary artery) graft to LAD is the gold-standard bypass graft — 90% patency at 10 years vs saphenous vein graft (50-60% at 10 years). PCI is preferred for: single or double vessel disease without left main/proximal LAD involvement, high surgical risk patients, and acute STEMI."]','A','Key rule: LEFT MAIN disease = CABG (not PCI) — the left main supplies >70% of LV myocardium; PCI failure risk is catastrophic. SYNTAX score >33 also favours CABG.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','medium','flashcard','Describe the anti-anginal drug ladder for stable angina management.','["Anti-anginal drug ladder: Step 1 — Short-acting nitrate (GTN sublingual) for acute episodes + aspirin 75mg OD + statin (all patients). Step 2 — Beta-blocker (atenolol 50-100mg OD, bisoprolol 5-10mg OD, or metoprolol 50-100mg BD) as first-line prophylaxis; reduces HR and myocardial O2 demand. If beta-blocker contraindicated (asthma, Prinzmetal''s, bradycardia) — use rate-limiting CCB (verapamil or diltiazem). Step 3 — Add dihydropyridine CCB (amlodipine 5-10mg OD) to beta-blocker; or switch to rate-limiting CCB if beta-blocker not tolerated. Step 4 — Add long-acting nitrate (isosorbide mononitrate) with nitrate-free interval ≥8h to avoid tolerance. Step 5 — Nicorandil, ranolazine, or ivabradine; refer for revascularisation."]','A','NITRATE TOLERANCE: Always prescribe long-acting nitrates with an 8-12 hour nitrate-free interval (usually overnight) to prevent tachyphylaxis — otherwise they lose efficacy within days.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','medium','flashcard','What are the criteria for a positive exercise stress test?','["Exercise stress test (EST) positive criteria: 1) ST depression ≥1mm (horizontal or downsloping) in ≥2 leads at 1mm past J-point — most specific criterion. 2) ST elevation ≥1mm in leads without Q waves (indicates transmural ischaemia). 3) Chest pain reproducing typical symptoms. 4) Significant ventricular arrhythmia (VT/VF during exercise). 5) Exercise-induced hypotension (SBP fall >10 mmHg despite increasing workload). High-risk EST features: early positive at <3 METs or Stage 1, ST changes in multiple leads, prolonged ST changes into recovery, Duke Treadmill Score ≤-11. Duke Treadmill Score = exercise time (min) - (5 × ST deviation) - (4 × angina index [0=none, 1=present, 2=limiting]). Score <-11 = high risk (annual mortality 5%)."]','A','Sensitivity of EST ~68%, specificity ~77% — it''s best used in intermediate pre-test probability patients; low or high pre-test probability patients should go straight to CT coronary angiography or stress echo/MRI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','medium','flashcard','What is Prinzmetal''s (vasospastic) angina and how does management differ from typical stable angina?','["Prinzmetal''s angina (variant/vasospastic angina): angina at REST (not exertion), usually occurring between midnight and 0800h, caused by focal coronary artery spasm rather than fixed atherosclerotic stenosis. ECG during episode: transient ST ELEVATION (not depression) that resolves with GTN. Confirmed by: coronary angiogram with provocative testing (ergonovine or acetylcholine). Treatment: 1) GTN for acute episodes. 2) Calcium channel blockers (amlodipine or diltiazem) — first-line for prevention (prevents spasm). 3) Long-acting nitrates as add-on. AVOID: beta-blockers (can worsen spasm by unopposed alpha-vasoconstriction) and aspirin at high doses (inhibits prostacyclin, a vasodilator). Triggers: smoking, cocaine, cold exposure, hyperventilation."]','A','Key distinguisher: Prinzmetal = ST ELEVATION at rest (not exercise); resolves spontaneously or with GTN — do not give thrombolysis without angiographic confirmation.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','medium','flashcard','What is the mechanism of ranolazine and in which patients is it particularly useful?','["Ranolazine mechanism: inhibits the late inward sodium current (late I-Na) in cardiomyocytes during ischaemia — reduces intracellular Na+ → reduces Ca2+ overload via Na/Ca exchanger → reduces diastolic wall tension and myocardial O2 demand. Does NOT affect heart rate or blood pressure (unlike beta-blockers and CCBs). Benefits: anti-anginal without haemodynamic effects, reduces HbA1c (modest anti-diabetic effect), and reduces ventricular arrhythmias. Useful in: 1) Patients with bradycardia or hypotension who cannot tolerate beta-blockers or CCBs. 2) Refractory angina on maximal other therapy. 3) Diabetic patients with angina. Caution: prolongs QT interval; avoid with other QT-prolonging drugs. Dose: 375-750mg BD."]','A','Ranolazine is the only anti-anginal that lowers HbA1c — useful in diabetic patients with CAD who still have angina on standard therapy.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','hard','flashcard','A 62-year-old man with stable angina on atenolol and amlodipine still has CCS Class III symptoms. What are the next management options and indications for coronary angiography?','["Next management: 1) Optimise existing drugs (maximise doses if tolerated — bisoprolol 10mg, amlodipine 10mg). 2) Add long-acting nitrate (ISMN 20-60mg OD with nitrate-free interval). 3) Consider ranolazine, nicorandil, or ivabradine as 3rd-line agents. 4) Refer for coronary angiography. Indications for coronary angiography in stable CAD: CCS III-IV despite optimal medical therapy, high-risk EST features (Duke score ≤-11, ST changes at <4 METs), imaging ischaemia >10% of LV myocardium, EF <40% with suspected CAD, occupational requirements (pilot, professional driver), post-resuscitation assessment. After angiography: PCI for 1-2 vessel disease; CABG for left main, triple vessel disease, or complex lesions (SYNTAX >22 with diabetes). Re-assess risk factors comprehensively."]','A','The threshold for angiography is NOT symptom severity alone — high-risk non-invasive findings (large ischaemic territory, LV dysfunction) take priority even in Class I-II symptoms.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','hard','flashcard','What is the pathophysiology of atherosclerosis and how does a stable plaque become an acute plaque rupture?','["Atherosclerosis pathophysiology: Initiation — endothelial injury (HTN, smoking, LDL, DM) → LDL enters intima → oxidised LDL attracts monocytes → foam cells (macrophages engorged with lipid) → fatty streak. Progression — foam cells die → necrotic lipid core forms; smooth muscle cells migrate from media → fibrous cap forms (plaque). Stable plaque = thick fibrous cap, small lipid core, low inflammatory activity. Vulnerable plaque = thin fibrous cap, large lipid core, high macrophage/T-cell infiltration. Plaque rupture: thin cap fissures (often at shoulder region) → subendothelial collagen + tissue factor exposed → platelet activation → thrombus formation → acute coronary syndrome. Statins stabilise plaques (reduce lipid core, increase cap thickness) regardless of baseline cholesterol level."]','A','The culprit lesion in most STEMIs was <50% stenosis on prior angiogram — it is PLAQUE VULNERABILITY (composition), not degree of stenosis, that determines rupture risk.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Stable Angina & CAD','hard','flashcard','What are the secondary prevention targets and drug therapy for a patient after PCI for stable angina?','["Secondary prevention post-PCI: 1) DUAL ANTIPLATELET: aspirin 75mg OD lifelong + clopidogrel 75mg OD for minimum 6 months after drug-eluting stent (DES) — do not stop early (stent thrombosis risk). 2) STATIN: high-intensity atorvastatin 40-80mg — target LDL <1.4 mmol/L (<55 mg/dL) per ESC 2021; if not achieved, add ezetimibe. 3) ACE INHIBITOR: if EF <40%, diabetes, or hypertension. 4) BETA-BLOCKER: if EF <40% or prior MI. 5) BP target <130/80 mmHg. 6) Smoking cessation (single most effective intervention — halves cardiovascular risk within 1 year). 7) HbA1c <53 mmol/mol in DM. 8) Cardiac rehabilitation. Avoid NSAIDs (increase MACE risk). Review adherence at every visit."]','A','Stopping clopidogrel prematurely after DES stenting is the leading cause of late stent thrombosis — always check for planned surgery or bleeding before any interruption of dual antiplatelet therapy.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','easy','flashcard','What are the NYHA functional classes for heart failure?','["NYHA Classification: Class I — No symptoms with ordinary activity; no limitation of physical activity. Class II — Symptoms (dyspnoea, fatigue) with moderate exertion (climbing stairs, walking uphill); slight limitation. Class III — Symptoms with minimal exertion (walking on level ground, dressing); marked limitation; comfortable at rest. Class IV — Symptoms at rest; unable to carry on any physical activity without discomfort. NYHA class guides therapy: Class II → ACE-I + beta-blocker; Class III-IV → add spironolactone, consider CRT-D/ICD; Class IV → consider inotropes, transplant. NYHA class can improve with treatment (unlike CCS which may be more fixed). Reassess NYHA class at every heart failure clinic visit."]','A','NYHA class is the primary tool for guiding HF therapy escalation and monitoring treatment response — it correlates with BNP levels and prognosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','easy','flashcard','List the Framingham criteria for heart failure diagnosis (major and minor).','["Framingham criteria — require 2 MAJOR or 1 MAJOR + 2 MINOR criteria for HF diagnosis. MAJOR: 1) Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea (PND) or orthopnoea. 2) Neck vein distension. 3) Rales (bibasal crackles). 4) Acute pulmonary oedema on CXR. 5) S3 gallop (third heart sound). 6) Cardiomegaly on CXR (CTR >0.5). 7) Hepatojugular reflux (JVP rises >4cm with abdominal pressure). 8) CVP >16 cm H2O. 9) Weight loss ≥4.5 kg in 5 days with diuretic treatment. MINOR: Bilateral ankle oedema, nocturnal cough, dyspnoea on ordinary exertion, hepatomegaly, pleural effusion, tachycardia (HR >120), weight loss <4.5 kg in 5 days. PND and orthopnoea are MAJOR — they suggest high LVEDP."]','A','S3 gallop is a MAJOR Framingham criterion — it is the most specific auscultatory finding for heart failure (ventricular filling sound indicating high LVEDP).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','easy','flashcard','What is the LMNOP mnemonic for acute pulmonary oedema management and the Ghana STG furosemide dose?','["LMNOP for acute pulmonary oedema: L — Lasix (furosemide): Ghana STG dose 40-80mg IV stat; repeat if poor response (max 200mg in first 2h in refractory cases). M — Morphine 2-4mg IV: reduces preload (venodilation), reduces anxiety and sympathetic drive. N — Nitrates: GTN sublingual or IV infusion (if SBP >90) — powerful venodilator, reduces preload. O — Oxygen: high-flow, target SpO2 ≥94%; CPAP/NIV if SpO2 not maintained. P — Positioning: sit patient upright (legs dependent reduces preload). Additional: urinary catheter to monitor output; restrict IV fluids; treat precipitant (AF, ACS, infection, medication non-adherence). Contraindications to nitrates: RV infarction, SBP <90, sildenafil use within 24h."]','A','Morphine is sometimes omitted in modern APO protocols due to association with worse outcomes in ADHF (ADHERE registry) — some guidelines now say GTN and furosemide first, morphine only for refractory distress.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','medium','flashcard','What BNP/NT-proBNP cutoffs differentiate heart failure from non-cardiac dyspnoea?','["BNP (B-type natriuretic peptide) and NT-proBNP are secreted by ventricular myocytes in response to wall stress (volume/pressure overload). Cutoffs for diagnosing HF in acute dyspnoea: BNP: <100 pg/mL = HF unlikely (NPV 90%); 100-400 = grey zone; >400 pg/mL = HF likely. NT-proBNP: <300 pg/mL = HF unlikely; age-adjusted cutoffs for positive: <50 yrs = >450; 50-75 yrs = >900; >75 yrs = >1800 pg/mL. Elevated causes other than HF: PE, RV failure, AF, CKD, sepsis, pneumonia (false positives). Low BNP despite HF: obesity (adipose tissue clears BNP), flash pulmonary oedema, constrictive pericarditis. Use BNP/NT-proBNP for diagnosis, not for routine monitoring of treated stable HF (ESC 2021)."]','A','BNP is best at RULING OUT HF — a BNP <100 in acute dyspnoea has >90% NPV. It cannot replace clinical judgement for positive diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','medium','flashcard','What distinguishes HFrEF from HFpEF and what are the evidence-based treatments for each?','["HFrEF (heart failure with reduced ejection fraction): EF <40%; systolic dysfunction; dilated LV; causes — ischaemic CM, DCM, myocarditis. Mortality-reducing drugs: ACE-I/ARB, beta-blocker (carvedilol/bisoprolol), mineralocorticoid antagonist (spironolactone), ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan replaces ACE-I — 20% further mortality reduction, PARADIGM-HF), SGLT2 inhibitor (dapagliflozin/empagliflozin — 25% HHF reduction). Device: ICD if EF <35% + NYHA II-III; CRT-D if EF <35% + LBBB + QRS ≥150ms. HFpEF (EF ≥50%): diastolic dysfunction; causes — HTN, DM, obesity, AF, HCM. No mortality-reducing drug proven (treat cause, diuretics for congestion, SGLT2i now showing benefit). HFmrEF (EF 40-49%) — emerging evidence for RAAS/beta-blocker/SGLT2i benefit."]','A','ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan) REPLACES ACE inhibitor in HFrEF — do NOT combine with ACE-I (angioedema risk). Washout period of 36 hours required when switching from ACE-I.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','medium','flashcard','What are the contraindications to starting an ACE inhibitor in heart failure and when should a beta-blocker be withheld?','["ACE inhibitor contraindications in HF: 1) SBP <90 mmHg (haemodynamic instability). 2) K+ >5.5 mmol/L (risk of hyperkalaemia). 3) Creatinine >220 μmol/L or eGFR <30 mL/min/1.73m² (relative). 4) Bilateral renal artery stenosis. 5) Pregnancy (teratogenic). 6) Prior angioedema to ACE-I. 7) Aortic stenosis (severe). Up to 30% rise in creatinine and 10-15% rise in K+ are acceptable after starting — do NOT stop for mild changes; check electrolytes at 1-2 weeks. Beta-blocker in HF: NEVER start during acute decompensation/wet/cold state (will worsen haemodynamics). Start only when patient is euvolaemic (''dry and warm'') — usually in community or at stable follow-up. Start at low dose (carvedilol 3.125mg BD) and titrate slowly over weeks."]','A','The classic error: starting a beta-blocker during acute decompensated HF — WAIT until the patient is dry and haemodynamically stable. Withdraw existing beta-blocker only if in cardiogenic shock.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','medium','flashcard','What are the indications for CRT (cardiac resynchronisation therapy) and ICD in heart failure?','["ICD indications in HF: EF ≤35% + NYHA Class II-III despite ≥3 months optimal medical therapy + expected survival >1 year. Prevents sudden cardiac death from VT/VF. Also indicated post-MI with EF <30% (SCD-HeFT trial). CRT (biventricular pacing) indications: EF ≤35% + NYHA Class III-IV + QRS ≥130ms (especially LBBB morphology ≥150ms) + sinus rhythm + optimal medical therapy ≥3 months. Resynchronises LV and RV contraction in LBBB — improves cardiac output without increasing O2 demand. CRT-D = CRT + ICD combined device. CRT benefits: reduces hospitalisation by 37%, mortality by 36%, improves NYHA class and 6-minute walk distance (CARE-HF, COMPANION trials). Right ventricular pacing alone worsens HF — avoid (risk of pacing-induced cardiomyopathy)."]','A','LBBB pattern with QRS ≥150ms responds BEST to CRT — RBBB or non-specific IVCD are poor predictors of response.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','hard','flashcard','Describe the haemodynamic Starling curve and how it is shifted in heart failure vs normal physiology.','["Frank-Starling Law: cardiac output increases as ventricular preload (LVEDV/LVEDP) increases — up to a point. Normal Starling curve: steep ascending limb; even slight preload increase markedly boosts CO; flat plateau at high preload. Heart failure Starling curve: shifted DOWN and to the RIGHT — same preload generates lower CO; plateau reached at lower preload (CO cannot increase with fluid). The depressed curve explains: fatigue (low CO), fluid retention (compensatory preload increase fails to restore CO). Interventions: Diuretics/vasodilators — move patient LEFT on the curve (reduce preload, reduce congestion, may slightly reduce CO). Inotropes (dobutamine) — shift curve UP (increases CO at any given preload). Understanding: in decompensated HF, aggressive diuresis without inotropic support can paradoxically drop BP if patient is on the flat part of the curve."]','A','The ''sweet spot'' in HF management is the optimal preload — too much causes pulmonary oedema, too little causes hypotension. BNP and daily weights track this balance.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','hard','flashcard','How is digoxin used in heart failure and AF, and what are the signs of toxicity?','["Digoxin in HF: Reduces hospitalisation (DIG trial) but does NOT reduce mortality. Indicated for: 1) HFrEF with AF — rate control (AV node blocking effect). 2) Systolic HFrEF with normal sinus rhythm refractory to ACE-I + beta-blocker (add-on, symptom improvement). Ghana STG: digoxin as 2nd-line for AF rate control after beta-blocker/diltiazem failure. Mechanism: inhibits Na/K-ATPase → intracellular Ca2+ increase → positive inotropy; also increases vagal tone (AV node slowing). Dose: 0.125-0.25mg OD; target level 0.5-0.9 ng/mL (not 1.0-2.0 old range — higher levels increase mortality in HFrEF). Toxicity signs (narrow therapeutic index): Nausea, vomiting, yellow/green visual halos, bradycardia, heart block. ECG: ''digoxin effect'' = scooped ST depression; toxicity = ventricular ectopics, bigeminy, AV block, atrial tachycardia with block. Treatment: stop digoxin, correct hypokalaemia/hypomagnesaemia, digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Digibind) for life-threatening toxicity."]','A','Hypokalaemia potentiates digoxin toxicity — always correct K+ before and during digoxin therapy. Target K+ 4.0-5.0 mmol/L.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Heart Failure','hard','flashcard','A 68-year-old with NYHA IV HFrEF (EF 20%) presents in cardiogenic shock. Outline the pharmacological management.','["Cardiogenic shock in HFrEF management: 1) INOTROPE: Dobutamine 2-20 mcg/kg/min IV — beta-1 agonist; increases contractility and CO; reduces SVR and PCWP (mild vasodilatory effect). First-line inotrope in low-output cardiogenic shock without profound hypotension. 2) VASOPRESSOR: If SBP <70 mmHg or refractory — add noradrenaline 0.1-2 mcg/kg/min to maintain perfusion pressure (target MAP >65). Avoid adrenaline (increases mortality — SOAP-II trial). 3) PHOSPHODIESTERASE INHIBITOR: Milrinone (PDE-3 inhibitor) — alternative inotrope/vasodilator; useful if beta-blockers on board (bypasses beta-receptors). 4) STOP nephrotoxins, withold ACE-I/ARB/beta-blocker temporarily in shock. 5) Treat precipitant (revascularise if ischaemia-driven). 6) Consider IABP, Impella, LVAD, transplant assessment. Monitor: arterial line, urinary catheter, lactate clearance, mixed venous O2 sat."]','A','Dobutamine increases cAMP via beta-1 stimulation; milrinone increases cAMP by inhibiting its breakdown — both are ''sensitisers'' but milrinone works even when beta-receptors are downregulated (as in chronic HF).','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','easy','flashcard','Classify atrial fibrillation by duration and describe the rate control strategy per Ghana STG.','["AF Classification by duration: Paroxysmal — self-terminating within 7 days (usually <48h); recurrent if ≥2 episodes. Persistent — lasts >7 days, requires pharmacological or electrical cardioversion. Long-standing persistent — continuous AF >12 months. Permanent — AF accepted by patient and physician; rhythm control no longer pursued. Ghana STG rate control drugs for AF: First-line — beta-blocker (atenolol 25-100mg OD, bisoprolol 2.5-10mg OD, or metoprolol 25-100mg BD); or rate-limiting CCB (verapamil 80-120mg TDS — do NOT combine with beta-blocker, risk of complete AV block). Second-line — digoxin 0.125-0.25mg OD (particularly in sedentary patients or with HF). Target resting HR: <110 bpm (lenient, RACE II trial); <80 bpm for symptomatic patients (strict). Do NOT use verapamil and beta-blocker together."]','A','Verapamil + beta-blocker combination = dangerous complete AV block. Never combine them for AF rate control — choose one or add digoxin instead.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','easy','flashcard','How is the CHA2DS2-VASc score calculated and when should anticoagulation be started in AF?','["CHA2DS2-VASc Score (anticoagulation in non-valvular AF): C — Congestive HF (1pt); H — Hypertension (1pt); A2 — Age ≥75 (2pts); D — Diabetes mellitus (1pt); S2 — prior Stroke/TIA/thromboembolism (2pts); V — Vascular disease (prior MI, PVD, aortic plaque) (1pt); A — Age 65-74 (1pt); Sc — Sex category female (1pt). Maximum = 9 points. Anticoagulation threshold: Men: score ≥1 → consider; score ≥2 → treat. Women: score ≥2 → consider; score ≥3 → treat. Anticoagulate with warfarin (target INR 2-3) or DOAC (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran — preferred if available). Valvular AF (rheumatic MS or prosthetic valve) requires WARFARIN ONLY — DOACs are NOT indicated for valvular AF. Assess bleeding risk with HAS-BLED score concurrently."]','A','FEMALE SEX is a risk modifier, not an independent risk factor — a woman with only female sex (score=1) does NOT need anticoagulation; she needs at least one other risk factor.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','easy','flashcard','What ECG features characterise Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome and which drugs are dangerous in WPW + AF?','["WPW ECG features (pre-excitation syndrome): 1) Short PR interval (<120ms) — early ventricular activation via accessory pathway (bundle of Kent). 2) Delta wave — slurred upstroke at onset of QRS (slow conduction through myocardium, not His-Purkinje). 3) Widened QRS (>120ms) — fusion of accessory and normal pathway conduction. 4) Secondary ST-T changes (opposite to delta wave direction). DANGEROUS DRUGS in WPW + AF: AVOID digoxin, verapamil, diltiazem, and adenosine — these slow AV node conduction but paradoxically increase conduction down the accessory pathway → very fast ventricular rate (>300 bpm) → ventricular fibrillation and sudden death. SAFE treatment for WPW + AF: procainamide IV (slows accessory pathway) or DC cardioversion. Definitive treatment: radiofrequency catheter ablation of accessory pathway."]','A','Mnemonic: DIVA drugs kill in WPW+AF — Digoxin, verapamIl, Verapamil, Adenosine. They accelerate accessory pathway conduction and can cause VF.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','medium','flashcard','What causes torsades de pointes and how is it treated?','["Torsades de pointes (TdP): polymorphic VT with QRS complexes that twist around the isoelectric baseline on ECG — associated with prolonged QT interval (QTc >500ms is high-risk). CAUSES of QT prolongation (congenital and acquired): Congenital — Romano-Ward (LQT1-3), Jervell-Lange-Nielsen (deafness). Acquired ABCDE: Antiarrhythmics (class IA: quinidine, procainamide; class III: sotalol, amiodarone), Antibiotics (azithromycin, fluoroquinolones, chloroquine — important in malaria-endemic regions like Ghana), antipsychotics/antidepressants (haloperidol, TCAs), hypoCaldaemia/hypoKalaemia/hypoMagnesaemia, Drugs (methadone, domperidone). TREATMENT: 1) IV Magnesium sulphate 2g over 1-2 minutes (drug of choice — shortens QT, suppresses TdP). 2) Correct hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia. 3) Temporary pacing (overdrive pacing) if recurrent. 4) Stop offending drug. DO NOT give additional class IA/III antiarrhythmics."]','A','Chloroquine and azithromycin BOTH prolong QT — the combination used historically in COVID-19 treatment caused TdP. Flag this risk in malaria-endemic settings where chloroquine is used.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','medium','flashcard','Describe the features and management of complete heart block (third-degree AV block).','["Complete heart block (CHB): complete dissociation between atrial and ventricular activity — P waves and QRS complexes have independent rates with no relationship. P rate: normal (60-100 bpm); QRS rate: escape rhythm — junctional (40-60 bpm, narrow QRS) if block at AV node, or ventricular (20-40 bpm, wide QRS) if infranodal. Causes: inferior MI (RCA supplies AV node — usually transient), anterior MI (LAD block — usually permanent), Lyme disease, sarcoidosis, amyloid, drugs (beta-blockers, digoxin, diltiazem), post-cardiac surgery. Symptoms: syncope (Stokes-Adams attacks), presyncope, bradycardia-related HF, cannon A waves in JVP (atria contracting against closed tricuspid valve). Treatment: 1) Atropine 0.5-1mg IV (only if junctional escape, not ventricular). 2) Temporary transvenous pacing (stabilise). 3) Permanent pacemaker — indicated for all symptomatic CHB and most asymptomatic CHB."]','A','Cannon A waves in JVP = pathognomonic of AV dissociation (complete heart block or VT) — atria contract against a closed tricuspid valve, creating giant venous waves.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','medium','flashcard','What vagal manoeuvres are used for SVT and when should adenosine be given?','["SVT (supraventricular tachycardia) management — stepwise: 1) VAGAL MANOEUVRES (increase parasympathetic tone → slow AV node): Valsalva manoeuvre (strain against closed glottis, then SUPINE LEG RAISE — modified Valsalva, 43% success); carotid sinus massage (right side first, contraindicated if carotid bruit); diving reflex (cold water on face). 2) ADENOSINE 6mg rapid IV bolus into large antecubital vein with saline flush — if no conversion, 12mg, then second 12mg. Mechanism: opens K+ channels in AV node → transient AV block → terminates re-entry circuit. Half-life: 10 seconds. Side effects: transient chest tightness, flushing, asystole (seconds). Contraindicated in: WPW+AF (see dangerous drugs), asthma (bronchospasm — use verapamil instead). 3) If adenosine fails: verapamil 5-10mg IV or DC cardioversion (unstable SVT)."]','A','Modified Valsalva (strain + supine leg raise) doubles conversion rate of SVT vs standard Valsalva — the leg raise increases venous return and augments the reflex.','universal',false,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','medium','flashcard','What energy levels are used for DC cardioversion and defibrillation in different arrhythmias?','["DC Cardioversion (synchronised — for organised rhythm): AF — start 120-200J biphasic (or 200J if monophasic); can escalate to 360J. Atrial flutter — 70-120J biphasic (lower energy, often converts first shock). VT (stable, with pulse) — 100-200J biphasic (synchronised). IMPORTANT: synchronise to QRS to avoid R-on-T (which triggers VF). Pre-cardioversion: anticoagulate if AF >48h or unknown duration (3 weeks warfarin OR TOE to exclude atrial thrombus). Fasting, conscious sedation. DC Defibrillation (UNsynchronised — for VF or pulseless VT): 200J biphasic immediately (do NOT synchronise — no organised rhythm to sync to). If VF persists: second shock 200-360J, then adrenaline 1mg IV every 3-5 min + amiodarone 300mg IV after 3rd shock (ALS algorithm). AED default energy is 150-200J biphasic."]','A','Cardioversion = SYNCHRONISED (for perfusing arrhythmias with QRS); Defibrillation = UNSYNCHRONISED (for VF/pulseless VT). Synchronising in VF is impossible and wastes time.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','hard','flashcard','Describe the rate vs rhythm control debate in AF management and which patients benefit from rhythm control.','["Rate control vs rhythm control in AF: RATE CONTROL: control ventricular rate (<110 bpm lenient, <80 bpm strict) with beta-blocker, diltiazem, or digoxin; do not attempt to restore sinus rhythm; anticoagulate per CHA2DS2-VASc. RHYTHM CONTROL: restore and maintain sinus rhythm using cardioversion + antiarrhythmic drugs (flecainide, propafenone for structurally normal hearts; amiodarone for structural heart disease or HF). AFFIRM trial (2002): no mortality benefit of rhythm over rate control in older patients with persistent AF. EAST-AFNET 4 trial (2020): EARLY rhythm control (within 1 year of AF diagnosis) in patients with significant cardiovascular conditions reduced stroke, cardiovascular death, and hospitalisation vs rate control. Who benefits from rhythm control: Young patients, first AF episode, symptomatic despite rate control, AF-induced cardiomyopathy (tachycardia-mediated), athletes, pre-excitation (WPW)."]','A','EAST-AFNET 4 changed the paradigm — EARLY rhythm control in newly diagnosed AF patients with cardiovascular risk factors reduces composite cardiovascular outcomes vs rate control alone.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','hard','flashcard','A 45-year-old with WPW presents with palpitations and HR 230 bpm with irregular rhythm on ECG. What does the ECG show and how do you manage it?','["ECG of WPW + AF: Very fast ventricular rate (often >200 bpm), IRREGULAR rhythm (confirms AF not AFL or SVT), and BROAD, bizarre QRS complexes with delta waves and fusion beats — mimics polymorphic VT. The irregular broad-complex tachycardia is the hallmark of pre-excited AF. MANAGEMENT: 1) If haemodynamically UNSTABLE (SBP <90, loss of consciousness, pulmonary oedema): immediate unsynchronised DC cardioversion 200J (do not delay for drugs). 2) If STABLE: IV procainamide 10mg/kg over 30 min (slows conduction in accessory pathway, may cardiovert). Alternative: ibutilide IV or flecainide IV. 3) AVOID: adenosine, digoxin, verapamil, diltiazem, beta-blockers — all increase accessory pathway conduction → VF risk. 4) Definitive: radiofrequency ablation of accessory pathway after stabilisation. Anti-coagulate per AF guidelines."]','A','Pre-excited AF = irregular BROAD complex tachycardia at >200 bpm — always think WPW. Treat as VF-equivalent if unstable; use procainamide not AV nodal blockers if stable.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Cardiac Arrhythmias','hard','flashcard','What are the indications and contraindications for amiodarone as an antiarrhythmic, and what are its major side effects?','["Amiodarone: Vaughan Williams class III antiarrhythmic — blocks K+ channels (main), also Na+, Ca2+ channels, and non-competitive beta-blockade (class I, II, IV properties). Uses: VT/VF (drug of choice in ALS algorithm — 300mg IV after 3rd shock), AF rhythm control in structural heart disease/HF (first-line in this group), SVT refractory to adenosine/verapamil, WPW arrhythmias (outpatient). CONTRAINDICATIONS: sinus bradycardia, SA node disease, AV block (unless pacemaker), thyroid disease (relative). Iodine content: 37% iodine by weight — causes thyrotoxicosis (hyperthyroid) or hypothyroidism. MAJOR SIDE EFFECTS: SITE mnemonic — Skin (photosensitivity, grey-blue discolouration), Iodine (thyroid dysfunction — hyper or hypo), Toxicity pulmonary (interstitial fibrosis, pneumonitis), Eyes (corneal microdeposits, optic neuropathy), liver toxicity, peripheral neuropathy. Monitor: TFTs, LFTs, CXR, PFTs, ophthalmology review every 6-12 months."]','A','Amiodarone has the LONGEST half-life of any drug (~40-55 days) — side effects persist for months after stopping. Drug interactions last months too (warfarin dose must be reduced by ~30-50% when amiodarone started).','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','easy','flashcard','What is the classic clinical triad of severe aortic stenosis and what is the prognosis after each symptom appears?','["Aortic stenosis (AS) classic triad — SAD: Syncope (exertional — failure to increase CO on exertion, or arrhythmia), Angina (supply-demand mismatch — LVH increases O2 demand, compressed subendocardial vessels reduce supply), Dyspnoea (HF — elevated LVEDP, pulmonary congestion; the LAST and worst symptom). Prognosis after symptom onset (natural history without surgery): Angina alone → mean survival 5 years. Syncope → mean survival 3 years. Heart failure → mean survival 1-2 years. AS murmur: harsh ejection systolic murmur (''diamond-shaped'' crescendo-decrescendo), loudest at right 2nd intercostal space (aortic area), radiates to bilateral carotids (characteristic ''bruit de diable''), with slow-rising small-volume pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus). Severe AS: valve area <1 cm², mean gradient >40 mmHg, Vmax >4 m/s."]','A','SAD mnemonic: Syncope (3yr), Angina (5yr), Dyspnoea/HF (1-2yr) — as each symptom appears, survival worsens. HF = worst prognosis; operate before this stage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','easy','flashcard','Describe the auscultatory findings of mitral stenosis and its most common complication.','["Mitral stenosis (MS) findings: OPENING SNAP (OS) — high-pitched click shortly after S2, caused by tensed, domed mitral leaflets opening abruptly. Shorter S2-OS interval = more severe MS (higher LA pressure closes mitral valve earlier). Murmur: LOW-PITCHED mid-diastolic RUMBLE with PRE-SYSTOLIC ACCENTUATION (in sinus rhythm), heard best at apex with patient in left lateral decubitus with bell of stethoscope. Loudest after exercise. Additional: loud S1, right ventricular tap (pulmonary hypertension), malar flush (''mitral facies''). Most common complication: ATRIAL FIBRILLATION — enlarged LA (from elevated LA pressure) triggers AF in 30-40% of patients; causes: loss of atrial kick → acute deterioration; risk of LA thrombus (especially in LAA) → stroke. Valve area: normal 4-6 cm²; moderate MS = 1-1.5 cm²; severe MS <1 cm²."]','A','S2-OS interval inversely relates to severity — the shorter the interval, the higher the LA pressure, the more severe the stenosis. A LONG S2-OS interval = mild MS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the auscultatory and peripheral signs of aortic regurgitation?','["Aortic regurgitation (AR) signs: Murmur: early DIASTOLIC decrescendo murmur, high-pitched blowing, heard best at left sternal edge 3rd intercostal space (LSE 3rd ICS) with patient sitting forward and breath held in expiration. Pulse: COLLAPSING (water-hammer) pulse — high-volume, rapidly rising and falling (wide pulse pressure: SBP high, DBP very low). Austin Flint murmur: mid-diastolic rumble at apex (AR jet pushes anterior mitral leaflet → relative mitral stenosis). Peripheral signs (all due to wide pulse pressure): De Musset''s sign (head nodding), Quincke''s sign (nail-bed pulsations), Traube''s sign (pistol-shot femoral bruit), Duroziez''s sign (to-and-fro femoral bruit with stethoscope pressure), Corrigan''s pulse (visible carotid pulsation). Causes: rheumatic (developing world), bicuspid aortic valve, infective endocarditis, aortic root dilation (Marfan''s, ankylosing spondylitis, syphilis)."]','A','Wide pulse pressure in AR is the haemodynamic hallmark — systolic BP high (stroke volume), diastolic BP low (backflow into LV). Normal pulse pressure = 40 mmHg; AR may show 60-100+ mmHg.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the mechanism of rheumatic valvular disease and which valve is most commonly affected?','["Rheumatic heart disease (RHD) mechanism: Group A beta-haemolytic Streptococcus (GAS) pharyngitis triggers autoimmune cross-reaction — streptococcal M protein antigens molecularly mimic cardiac proteins (valvular, myocardial, sarcolemma). T-lymphocytes and antibodies attack cardiac tissue → pancarditis → valve scarring. Aschoff bodies = pathognomonic granulomatous lesions in myocardium. Valve involvement order (MOST common first): Mitral > Aortic > Tricuspid > Pulmonary (MAP — Mary ate terrible pizza). Mitral stenosis is the MOST COMMON manifestation of chronic RHD. Consequences: leaflet thickening, fibrosis, calcification, fusion of commissures (stenosis), retraction and scarring of chordae (regurgitation). Primary prevention: penicillin for GAS pharyngitis. Secondary prevention: long-acting penicillin IM (benzathine penicillin G) every 3-4 weeks for years — duration depends on degree of cardiac involvement."]','A','Ghana and sub-Saharan Africa have HIGH RHD burden — always consider RHD in young patients with mitral stenosis or aortic regurgitation. Secondary penicillin prophylaxis is critical.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','medium','flashcard','Describe the murmur of mitral regurgitation and distinguish acute from chronic MR.','["Mitral regurgitation (MR) murmur: HOLOSYSTOLIC (pan-systolic) murmur, high-pitched blowing, heard best at APEX, radiates to the LEFT AXILLA (posterior MR jet) or to the left sternal edge (anterior jet). Does not vary with respiration. Accentuated by handgrip (increases afterload → more regurgitant volume). Chronic MR: LV dilates (volume overload) → eccentric hypertrophy; LV enlargement on CXR/echo; LA enlargement; usually well-tolerated for years; soft S1 (reduced mitral leaflet coaptation). Acute MR (papillary muscle rupture, IE, chordal rupture): sudden massive regurgitation into non-dilated LA → acute pulmonary oedema; haemodynamically catastrophic; murmur may be short (LA pressure equalises quickly — early termination of gradient); requires emergency surgery. Indications for surgery in MR: EF <60% or LVESD >45mm (act before LV deteriorates), symptomatic severe MR."]','A','In ACUTE MR, the murmur may be SOFT or SHORT because the LA rapidly equilibrates with the LV — always suspect acute MR in a patient with new pulmonary oedema and a soft systolic murmur.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the echocardiographic parameters that define severe aortic stenosis and when is surgery indicated?','["Severe AS echocardiographic parameters: 1) Peak aortic jet velocity (Vmax) ≥4 m/s. 2) Mean pressure gradient ≥40 mmHg. 3) Aortic valve area (AVA) <1.0 cm² (or <0.6 cm²/m² body surface area = indexed AVA). 4) Velocity ratio (dimensionless index) <0.25. Low-flow low-gradient AS: EF <50% + AVA <1cm² + mean gradient <40 mmHg — pseudo-severe vs truly severe: dobutamine stress echo to unmask true gradient. Surgical AVR (SAVR) indications: Severe symptomatic AS + surgical risk acceptable. Severe AS + EF <50%. Severe AS undergoing other cardiac surgery. Asymptomatic severe AS with very high Vmax (>5 m/s) or rapid progression. TAVI (transcatheter AVR) indications: Severe symptomatic AS + high/prohibitive surgical risk OR age >75 with intermediate risk (PARTNER trial). TAVI now preferred for most patients >75 years with favourable anatomy."]','A','AVA ALONE cannot diagnose severity — always integrate with gradient. Low-flow low-gradient severe AS (AVA<1cm², gradient<40mmHg) occurs in HF with reduced EF; dobutamine stress echo is the key test.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the features of mitral valve prolapse (MVP) and when does it require treatment?','["Mitral valve prolapse (MVP): billowing of one or both mitral valve leaflets ≥2mm above the mitral annular plane into the left atrium during systole — most common valvular abnormality (2-3% prevalence); autosomal dominant; associated with Marfan''s syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. Auscultation: mid-systolic CLICK (billowing leaflet tensing chordae) followed by a late systolic murmur (if MR present). Dynamic auscultation: Standing/Valsalva (reduces preload) → click moves EARLIER; Squatting/Leg raise (increases preload) → click moves LATER. Complications (mostly in classic MVP with thick myxomatous leaflets >5mm): MR (progressive), chordal rupture (acute MR), infective endocarditis, stroke (thromboembolic), arrhythmias, sudden cardiac death (rare). Treatment: MR monitoring by echo every 3-5 years; surgical repair if severe symptomatic MR (repair preferred over replacement); aspirin if prior TIA."]','A','Memory aid: Smaller LV (standing, Valsalva) = click earlier; Bigger LV (squatting, leg raise) = click later. The click timing is the MVP dynamic auscultation gold standard.','universal',false,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','hard','flashcard','How does the management of chronic aortic regurgitation differ from acute AR, and what are surgical indications?','["Chronic AR management: Medical — vasodilators (amlodipine or ACE inhibitors) to reduce afterload and regurgitant fraction; manage BP (diastolic hypertension worsens AR); annual echo surveillance. Surgical indications (AVR): 1) SYMPTOMATIC severe AR (any symptom — dyspnoea, angina, reduced exercise tolerance). 2) ASYMPTOMATIC severe AR with: LV EF <50%, LVESD >50mm, LVEDD >65mm with low surgical risk, or patient undergoing other cardiac surgery. Acute AR management: HAEMODYNAMIC EMERGENCY — acute massive volume overload in non-dilated LV → precipitous rise in LVEDP → acute pulmonary oedema + cardiogenic shock. NO TIME for chronic compensation. Medical bridge: vasodilators + inotropes (dobutamine) to lower PCWP + increase CO; IABP CONTRAINDICATED in AR (diastolic augmentation worsens regurgitation). Treatment: EMERGENCY SURGERY (valve replacement). Temporary aortic valvuloplasty not effective."]','A','IABP (intra-aortic balloon pump) is CONTRAINDICATED in AR — diastolic inflation increases aortic root pressure, massively worsening the regurgitant volume. Remember this contraindication.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','hard','flashcard','A 28-year-old Ghanaian woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis in AF develops worsening dyspnoea in pregnancy. What are the management priorities?','["Rheumatic MS in pregnancy: Physiological changes — increased plasma volume (40-50%), increased CO (30-50%), tachycardia (all worsening MS haemodynamics); can precipitate acute pulmonary oedema, especially in 2nd trimester and delivery. Management: 1) RATE CONTROL: reduce HR to optimise diastolic filling time — use beta-1 selective beta-blocker (metoprolol) or digoxin (safe in pregnancy); target HR <90 bpm. 2) DIURETICS: furosemide for pulmonary congestion (safe). 3) ANTICOAGULATION: if AF present — warfarin is preferred for MS (DOAC NOT indicated in valvular AF); discuss teratogenicity risk (warfarin embryopathy in 1st trimester — switch to LMWH in weeks 6-12). 4) If haemodynamically unstable despite medical therapy: PERCUTANEOUS MITRAL BALLOON VALVOTOMY (PMBV) can be performed in 2nd trimester with radiation shielding — reduces valve gradient without open surgery. 5) Delivery: planned vaginal delivery with epidural preferred; avoid tachycardia triggers."]','A','PMBV (balloon valvuloplasty) is the intervention of choice in pregnant MS — it avoids cardiopulmonary bypass which has high fetal loss risk. Optimal timing: 2nd trimester.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Valvular Heart Disease','hard','flashcard','What is the difference between mechanical and bioprosthetic heart valves and what anticoagulation is required for each?','["Mechanical prosthetic valves: longer durability (30+ years, no structural deterioration); require lifelong anticoagulation with warfarin (DOACs are INFERIOR for mechanical valves — RE-ALIGN trial showed harm). Target INR: mitral position 2.5-3.5; aortic position 2.0-3.0 (add aspirin 75mg if thromboembolism despite therapeutic INR). Bioprosthetic valves (biological — bovine pericardium or porcine): no lifelong anticoagulation required (aspirin 3 months then consider stopping); limited durability (10-15 years in older patients, shorter in younger — increased structural valve degeneration under 65 years old); preferred in elderly (>65-70 yrs) or those with contraindication to warfarin, women of childbearing age. Valve choice factors: patient age, anticoagulation ability/compliance, pregnancy plans, patient preference, valve position (mitral requires higher INR), physician/centre experience. Prosthetic valve endocarditis: high mortality; treat with at least 6 weeks IV antibiotics + low threshold for surgery."]','A','DOACs are CONTRAINDICATED for mechanical prosthetic valves — warfarin remains the only proven anticoagulant. This is one of the few remaining absolute warfarin-only indications.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','easy','flashcard','What are the Modified Duke Criteria for diagnosing infective endocarditis?','["Modified Duke Criteria — DEFINITE IE: 2 MAJOR criteria OR 1 MAJOR + 3 MINOR criteria OR 5 MINOR criteria. MAJOR CRITERIA: 1) Positive blood cultures — typical IE organism in ≥2 separate cultures (Strep viridans, Strep gallolyticus, HACEK group, Staph aureus, Enterococcus) OR persistently positive BCs ≥12h apart OR 3/3 or majority of ≥4 cultures positive. 2) Echocardiographic evidence — oscillating intracardiac mass (vegetation), perivalvular abscess, new partial dehiscence of prosthetic valve, or new valvular regurgitation. MINOR CRITERIA: 1) Predisposing cardiac condition or IVDU. 2) Fever ≥38°C. 3) Vascular phenomena (emboli, mycotic aneurysm, Janeway lesions, conjunctival haemorrhage). 4) Immunological phenomena (Osler nodes, Roth spots, positive rheumatoid factor, GN). 5) Microbiological evidence not meeting major criteria."]','A','Mnemonic for MAJOR Duke: 2Bs — Bugs (positive blood cultures) + Bleep (echocardiographic evidence). Both are MAJOR; all others are MINOR.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','easy','flashcard','What organism is most common in native valve endocarditis in non-IV drug users, and what is the empiric treatment?','["Native valve endocarditis (NVE) — community-acquired in non-IVDU: Most common organism: Streptococcus viridans (oral streptococci — alpha-haemolytic) following dental procedures or from dental bacteraemia. Other NVE organisms: Enterococcus faecalis (bowel/urinary procedures), Staph aureus (increasingly common — associated with skin infections, catheter use). Empiric treatment (NVE, community-acquired, non-IVDU): Benzylpenicillin 1.2g IV 4-hourly + gentamicin 1mg/kg IV TDS for 4-6 weeks. If penicillin-allergic: vancomycin IV + gentamicin. If Staph aureus suspected or IVDU: flucloxacillin 2g IV 4-hourly + gentamicin (or vancomycin if MRSA risk). Duration: 4 weeks for most NVE (Strep viridans); 6 weeks for Staph aureus or prosthetic valve IE. Always get 3 sets of blood cultures from different sites BEFORE starting antibiotics."]','A','Take ≥3 blood culture sets from DIFFERENT venepuncture sites BEFORE antibiotics — timing is less critical than site variation. Cultures taken over 12-24h improve sensitivity.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','easy','flashcard','What organism is most common in IV drug user endocarditis and which valve is typically affected?','["IV drug user (IVDU) endocarditis: Most common organism: Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA) — from skin flora at injection sites. Valve affected: TRICUSPID VALVE — most common in IVDU endocarditis (right-sided IE, from venous injection sites). Contrast with non-IVDU: LEFT-SIDED (mitral > aortic). Clinical features of right-sided IE: septic PULMONARY EMBOLI (multiple nodular opacities on CXR), haemoptysis, pleuritic chest pain, absence of left-sided peripheral stigmata (no finger clubbing, splinter haemorrhages less common). Echocardiography: tricuspid vegetation with tricuspid regurgitation (TR). Treatment: flucloxacillin 2g IV 4-hourly (MSSA) OR vancomycin IV (MRSA or penicillin allergy) for 4-6 weeks. Right-sided IE has BETTER prognosis than left-sided; surgical threshold higher (surgery rarely needed if responding to antibiotics)."]','A','IVDU = TRICUSPID valve + Staph aureus. Pulmonary septic emboli (bilateral nodular infiltrates on CXR) are the hallmark of right-sided IE — think IVDU in any young patient with this pattern.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','medium','flashcard','What are the peripheral stigmata of infective endocarditis and what causes each?','["Peripheral stigmata of IE — mechanism: Osler nodes: PAINFUL, tender nodules on fingertips and toe pads — due to immune complex deposition (vasculitis), NOT emboli. Janeway lesions: PAINLESS, flat erythematous/haemorrhagic macules on palms and soles — septic emboli (microabscesses in dermis). Splinter haemorrhages: longitudinal dark brown streaks under nails — microemboli in nail-bed capillaries; also seen in trauma (non-IE splinter haemorrhages common in manual workers). Roth spots: oval retinal haemorrhages with white centres — immune complex vasculitis in retinal vessels. Clubbing: chronic IE (>6 weeks) — periosteal proliferation. Conjunctival haemorrhages (Roth spots of conjunctiva): subconjunctival petechia. Mnemonic: Osler = immunOlogical (painful); Janeway = embolic (painless). ''O'' for both painful and immunological."]','A','OsLer = Lesions that hurt (immune); Janeway = painless + palms/soles (embolic). Classic exam question: distinguish these two peripheral signs.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','medium','flashcard','What are the HACEK organisms and what clinical features suggest their involvement in IE?','["HACEK group: slow-growing gram-negative bacteria that cause 5-10% of community-acquired NVE. Organisms: H — Haemophilus aphrophilus/parainfluenzae; A — Aggregatibacter (formerly Actinobacillus); C — Cardiobacterium hominis; E — Eikenella corrodens; K — Kingella kingae. All are normal oral/upper respiratory flora. Clinical features: subacute presentation (weeks-months), large vegetations (embolic risk), frequently cause culture-negative endocarditis on standard BCs (require prolonged incubation ≥2 weeks or specific media). Associated with poor dental hygiene, prior dental work. Large vegetations → high embolic risk (especially CNS, spleen, renal). Treatment: ceftriaxone 2g IV OD for 4 weeks (amoxicillin-clavulanate alternative). Typically penicillin-sensitive historically, but increasing beta-lactamase production noted. Culture-negative IE also caused by: Coxiella burnetti (Q fever), Bartonella, Brucella — serological testing required."]','A','HACEK are responsible for most LARGE vegetations in community IE — large vegetations (>10mm) have highest embolic risk and lower surgical threshold.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','medium','flashcard','What are the indications for dental prophylaxis against infective endocarditis?','["Dental prophylaxis indications (NICE/AHA/ESC guidelines): HIGH-RISK cardiac conditions requiring prophylaxis: 1) Prosthetic heart valves (mechanical or biological) or valve repair with prosthetic material. 2) Previous infective endocarditis. 3) Congenital heart disease (CHD) — ONLY: unrepaired cyanotic CHD; repaired CHD with prosthetic material (within 6 months of procedure); repaired CHD with residual defects at or adjacent to prosthetic material. Cardiac conditions NOT requiring prophylaxis: isolated MVP, aortic stenosis/regurgitation/mitral regurgitation without prosthesis, repaired simple VSD/ASD, CABG, coronary stents, cardiac pacemakers. Antibiotic regimen: amoxicillin 3g PO (adults) 30-60 min before procedure (or clindamycin 600mg if penicillin-allergic). PROCEDURES requiring prophylaxis: all dental procedures involving gingival manipulation or mucosa perforation (not simple check-up or radiograph)."]','A','NICE UK (2008) removed prophylaxis for most patients — however ESC and AHA still recommend it for HIGH-RISK cardiac conditions. Know the HIGH-RISK list: prosthetic valve, prior IE, complex congenital HD.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','medium','flashcard','What organism causes early prosthetic valve endocarditis and how does it differ from late PVE?','["Prosthetic valve endocarditis (PVE): Early PVE (<60 days after surgery): contaminant during surgery or perioperative bacteraemia. Organisms: Staphylococcus epidermidis (coagulase-negative Staph, #1 in early PVE), Staph aureus (including MRSA), gram-negative bacilli (Enterobacteriaceae), Candida. Late PVE (>60 days): similar organism profile to NVE — Strep viridans, Staph aureus, Enterococcus. Intermediate (60 days-1 year): mixed profile. Treatment: Early PVE — vancomycin + rifampicin + gentamicin (empirical, covers MRSA + biofilm); Late PVE — treat as NVE per organism. Rifampicin is crucial for prosthetic valve IE — penetrates biofilm on prosthetic material; do NOT start rifampicin until blood cultures clear (prevents selection of rifampicin-resistant mutants). PVE generally requires longer treatment (6 weeks) and has higher surgical rate than NVE."]','A','Rifampicin penetrates biofilm on prosthetic valves — it is ESSENTIAL in PVE regimens. But start it only after 3-5 days of bacteriostatic antibiotics (once bacteraemia controlled) to prevent rifampicin resistance.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','hard','flashcard','What are the surgical indications in infective endocarditis and when should surgery not be delayed?','["Surgical indications in IE — URGENT (within 24-48h): 1) Heart failure due to valve dysfunction (MR or AR with acute pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock — most common indication). 2) Uncontrolled infection: abscess, fistula, penetrating lesion, or infection with resistant organisms (fungi, MRSA with persistent bacteraemia, Brucella, Q fever). 3) Prevention of embolism: vegetation >10mm with major embolic episode DESPITE antibiotic therapy; vegetation >15mm regardless. EARLY SURGERY (within 1-2 weeks): Persistent vegetation after systemic embolism; vegetation >10mm (high embolic risk); progression of valve destruction on echo. CONTRAINDICATIONS to surgery: severe stroke with cerebral haemorrhage (wait ≥4 weeks); poor surgical candidate (overwhelming comorbidity); uncomplicated Strep viridans NVE responding to antibiotics. Post-surgery: continue antibiotics for full course (count from first negative BC post-surgery)."]','A','Heart failure is the #1 surgical indication in IE — urgent surgery reduces mortality compared to antibiotics alone when HF is present. Do not delay for ''completing antibiotics first.''','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','hard','flashcard','A 32-year-old with a history of IVDU presents with fever, new murmur, and haematuria. Blood cultures grow MRSA. Describe the full management.','["Management of MRSA IE: 1) BLOOD CULTURES: 3 sets before antibiotics (already done by history). 2) ANTIBIOTICS: Vancomycin IV (target trough 15-20 mg/L or AUC/MIC guided dosing) as the cornerstone for MRSA. Duration: minimum 6 weeks for Staph aureus bacteraemia + IE. ADD daptomycin if vancomycin MIC ≥2 (MIC creep/vancomycin-resistant). 3) ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY: transthoracic echo (TTE) urgently; if tricuspid vegetation suspected — TTE often adequate for right-sided; transoesophageal echo (TOE) for left-sided, prosthetic valves, or if TTE non-diagnostic (sensitivity 95% vs 60-70% TTE). 4) COMPLICATION SEARCH: haematuria → renal emboli or GN (immune complex); CT abdomen/pelvis for splenic abscess; MRI brain for silent CNS emboli. 5) HAEMATURIA management: urinalysis + creatinine (embolic vs GN). 6) SURGERY: if haemodynamic compromise, abscess, or emboli despite antibiotics. 7) ADDRESS IVDU: substance misuse counselling, naltrexone, harm reduction."]','A','Staph aureus bacteraemia always requires echocardiography — the 23% rate of IE in Staph aureus bacteraemia makes empiric echo mandatory, regardless of clinical features.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Infective Endocarditis','hard','flashcard','What are the neurological complications of infective endocarditis and how do they influence surgical timing?','["Neurological complications of IE: 1) Cardioembolic stroke (most common) — vegetation fragment embolises to cerebral artery; 15-35% of IE patients; silent emboli on MRI in up to 50%. 2) Cerebral mycotic aneurysm — septic emboli infect arterial wall → aneurysm formation → SAH risk; detected by CTA/MRA or conventional angiography. 3) Meningitis — haematogenous bacterial meningitis; CSF shows organism on culture. 4) Brain abscess — septic emboli seed brain parenchyma. 5) Cerebral haemorrhage — haemorrhagic transformation of embolic stroke or ruptured mycotic aneurysm. Surgical timing after neurological complication: Ischaemic stroke WITHOUT haemorrhage: surgery can proceed urgently if HF/uncontrolled infection; brain benefit of delaying surgery questionable if neurological deficit is stable. Haemorrhagic stroke or intracranial haemorrhage: DELAY SURGERY ≥4 weeks (or ≥1 month) to allow brain healing; risk of re-bleeding on cardiopulmonary bypass. Mycotic aneurysm: neurosurgical/endovascular treatment BEFORE cardiac surgery."]','A','Intracranial haemorrhage = delay cardiac surgery ≥4 weeks. Ischaemic stroke alone (without haemorrhage) does NOT mandate surgery delay if cardiac indications are urgent.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','easy','flashcard','Name the 3 components of Beck''s triad in cardiac tamponade.','["Beck''s Triad of cardiac tamponade: 1) HYPOTENSION — reduced cardiac output from compression of all four cardiac chambers by pericardial fluid (impaired diastolic filling → reduced stroke volume). 2) ELEVATED JVP (distended neck veins) — impaired venous return due to external cardiac compression; JVP does NOT fall with inspiration (Kussmaul''s sign present in constrictive pericarditis, inconsistent in tamponade). 3) MUFFLED (quiet) HEART SOUNDS — large pericardial effusion attenuates cardiac sound transmission. Additional features: PULSUS PARADOXUS (exaggerated inspiratory fall in SBP >10 mmHg — pathognomonic), tachycardia, cold clammy peripheries. ECG: ELECTRICAL ALTERNANS (alternating QRS axis/voltage due to swinging heart in fluid) + sinus tachycardia + low voltage. Urgent pericardiocentesis is life-saving."]','A','ELECTRICAL ALTERNANS on ECG (beat-to-beat variation in QRS axis/height) is PATHOGNOMONIC of large pericardial effusion with tamponade — if you see this on ECG, suspect tamponade immediately.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the causes and ECG findings of acute pericarditis?','["Acute pericarditis causes: VIRAL most common (60-70%) — Coxsackie B, Echo virus, CMV, EBV, HIV; Bacterial — TB pericarditis (most common cause in sub-Saharan Africa and Ghana); Autoimmune — SLE, RA, Scleroderma, Dressler''s syndrome; Uraemic pericarditis (CKD); Neoplastic (lung, breast metastases, lymphoma); Post-radiation; Idiopathic. Clinical: sharp pleuritic chest pain worse on LYING FLAT and inspiration, relieved by SITTING FORWARD (pathognomonic position); pericardial friction rub (scratchy, superficial, 3-component: systolic, early diastolic, pre-systolic). ECG in acute pericarditis (CLASSIC 4-stage evolution): Stage 1 — diffuse concave-upward (saddle-shaped) ST elevation in MULTIPLE leads (I, II, aVF, V2-V6) + PR DEPRESSION (highly specific). Stage 2 — ST returns to baseline, T waves flatten. Stage 3 — T wave inversions (widespread). Stage 4 — ECG normalises."]','A','PR DEPRESSION in pericarditis (not seen in STEMI) is the most SPECIFIC ECG finding — it reflects subepicardial inflammation of the atria. Look for it to confirm pericarditis vs STEMI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','easy','flashcard','What is the treatment of acute pericarditis per Ghana STG 2017?','["Acute pericarditis treatment per Ghana STG 2017: 1) IBUPROFEN 400-800mg TDS for 1-2 weeks (first-line NSAID — also helps effusion resorption). 2) COLCHICINE 0.5mg BD for 3 months — added to NSAID; reduces recurrence by 50% (COPE trial); must be added even on first episode. 3) Gastroprotection: omeprazole 20mg OD while on NSAIDs. 4) RESTRICT PHYSICAL ACTIVITY — until symptom-free + CRP normalised (athletes: complete restriction until symptom-free and CRP/echo normalised). STEROIDS: AVOID in first-line acute pericarditis (increase recurrence risk). Steroids ONLY for: uraemic pericarditis (haemodialysis first), connective tissue disease pericarditis (SLE, RA), steroid-dependent recurrent pericarditis. Aspirin preferred over ibuprofen in post-MI pericarditis (Dressler''s) — ibuprofen impairs myocardial healing. Treat underlying cause (e.g., ATT for TB pericarditis)."]','A','Colchicine HALVES recurrence rate — ALWAYS add it at first episode, not just for recurrent pericarditis. The COPE and ICAP trials established this as standard of care.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','How does pericarditis differ from STEMI on ECG, and how do you differentiate clinically?','["Pericarditis vs STEMI ECG differentiation: Pericarditis: DIFFUSE ST elevation (multiple territory leads: inferior + anterior + lateral simultaneously); SADDLE-SHAPED (concave upward) ST elevation; PR DEPRESSION (pathognomonic); NO RECIPROCAL ST depression (key); ST:T ratio in V6 >0.25 (Spodick''s sign). STEMI: ST elevation in SINGLE coronary territory (inferior, anterior, or lateral — not all simultaneously); CONVEX (domed) ST elevation; reciprocal ST DEPRESSION in opposite leads (e.g., inferior STEMI → ST depression in V1-V4); evolves to Q waves + T inversions within hours. Clinical differentiation: Pericarditis pain: sharp, pleuritic, positional (worse supine, better sitting forward), may have fever. STEMI pain: crushing central pressure, non-pleuritic, radiates to jaw/arm, diaphoresis, pale, autonomic features. Troponin can be elevated in pericarditis (myopericarditis) — does NOT alone confirm MI."]','A','The KEY ECG differentiator: RECIPROCAL CHANGES = STEMI; DIFFUSE + CONCAVE ST with PR depression = PERICARDITIS. Absence of reciprocal changes is the most reliable pericarditis indicator.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','Define pulsus paradoxus and explain its mechanism in cardiac tamponade.','["Pulsus paradoxus: exaggerated inspiratory fall in systolic BP >10 mmHg (normal inspiratory fall ≤10 mmHg). Measurement: inflate BP cuff above systolic, slowly deflate — note pressure where Korotkoff sounds first heard (expiration only) vs pressure where sounds heard throughout; difference >10 mmHg = pulsus paradoxus. Mechanism in tamponade: VENTRICULAR INTERDEPENDENCE — pericardial fluid constrains total cardiac volume. Inspiration → increased venous return to RV → RV expands → interventricular septum shifts LEFT → LV filling impaired → LV stroke volume falls → SBP falls. In normal hearts, pericardium is compliant enough to accommodate this; in tamponade, fixed pericardial volume makes both ventricles compete. Other causes of pulsus paradoxus: constrictive pericarditis (inconsistent), severe asthma/COPD (large intrathoracic pressure swings), massive PE, tension pneumothorax, severe hypovolaemia. Absent in: pericarditis alone (no effusion), ASD (equalises pressures)."]','A','Pulsus paradoxus is ABSENT in aortic regurgitation despite tamponade (pulsus bisferiens masks it) and in ASD (both sides communicate and equalise). These are rare but classic exceptions.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','What is Kussmaul''s sign and in which conditions is it found?','["Kussmaul''s sign: PARADOXICAL RISE in JVP during INSPIRATION (normally JVP falls on inspiration due to increased venous return to right heart). Mechanism: in constrictive pericarditis, the rigid pericardium cannot expand to accommodate the inspiratory increase in venous return → RV filling rises, but pericardium prevents volume increase → JVP rises instead of falling. Found in: 1) CONSTRICTIVE PERICARDITIS — classic and most common cause. 2) Restrictive cardiomyopathy. 3) Right ventricular infarction. 4) Tricuspid stenosis. 5) Pericardial effusion with tamponade (inconsistent — JVP usually high and non-phasic). Distinguished from pulsus paradoxus: Kussmaul''s sign = JVP rises on inspiration; Pulsus paradoxus = SBP falls on inspiration. They can coexist. Constrictive pericarditis: causes — TB (#1 in Africa/Ghana), post-viral, post-radiation, post-surgery."]','A','Kussmaul''s sign = JVP goes UP on breathing IN — the OPPOSITE of normal. Only in constrictive pericarditis and RV infarction. Remember: ''Kussmaul'' sounds like ''KUSSS'' — inspiration squeezes JVP UP.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','medium','flashcard','How do you differentiate constrictive pericarditis from restrictive cardiomyopathy?','["Constrictive pericarditis vs restrictive cardiomyopathy — difficult and clinically crucial distinction: SHARED FEATURES: elevated JVP, Kussmaul''s sign, normal/reduced LV cavity, marked diastolic dysfunction, ascites/oedema. CONSTRICTIVE PERICARDITIS features: pericardial knock (early S3-like sound from abrupt diastolic filling stop); pericardial calcification on CXR/CT (especially TB); CT/MRI — thickened pericardium (>4mm); echo — septal bounce (interventricular dependence); tissue Doppler: E'' >8 cm/s (myocardium is normal — bounce off stiff pericardium); respiratory variation in mitral E wave >25% (ventricular interdependence). RESTRICTIVE CARDIOMYOPATHY features: no pericardial thickening; tissue Doppler E'' <8 cm/s (intrinsic myocardial disease); BNP often higher; may have LVH, bright myocardium (amyloid); biopsy shows infiltrate. Gold standard: cardiac catheterisation (dip-and-plateau/square root sign in both — less helpful) vs CT showing pericardial thickness."]','A','Tissue Doppler E'' >8 cm/s = constrictive (normal myocardium); E'' <8 cm/s = restrictive (diseased myocardium). This single measurement is the most discriminating non-invasive test.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','hard','flashcard','A 42-year-old Ghanaian man presents with massive pericardial effusion and JVP +6 cm. Echo shows RV collapse. Describe the emergency management of cardiac tamponade.','["Cardiac tamponade management: EMERGENCY — do not delay for investigations beyond urgent echo (if already suspected). 1) POSITION: sit patient upright (30-45°) — optimises venous return and cardiac filling. 2) IV FLUID CHALLENGE: 500mL crystalloid bolus — transiently improves CO by increasing preload (bridge to definitive treatment); useful if hypotensive. 3) AVOID VASODILATORS, DIURETICS, BETA-BLOCKERS — all reduce preload/CO and can cause cardiovascular collapse. 4) PERICARDIOCENTESIS — definitive emergency treatment: subxiphoid approach (needle at 45° angle between xiphisternum and left costal margin, directed toward left shoulder); drain under echo guidance (aspirating even 20-50mL can dramatically improve haemodynamics); send pericardial fluid for cytology, culture, glucose, protein, pH, LDH. 5) If echo shows dense clot/haematoma (post-surgical, trauma) → surgical drainage (pericardial window). Ghana context: always send for TB culture + AFB smear (most common cause of massive effusion in sub-Saharan Africa)."]','A','Only 20-50mL of pericardial fluid removal can reverse tamponade — the pressure-volume relationship of the pericardium is steep; small volume reduction = large pressure drop.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','hard','flashcard','What are the imaging findings and haemodynamic features of constrictive pericarditis?','["Constrictive pericarditis imaging and haemodynamics: CHEST X-RAY: pericardial calcification (''eggshell'' calcium ring around heart — classic in TB or prior viral constrictive); cardiomegaly absent (small-normal heart despite HF). CT/MRI: pericardial thickening >4mm; calcification (CT better than MRI for calcium); MRI shows pericardial enhancement and tethering. ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY: thickened/calcified pericardium; septal bounce (interventricular septal ''dance'' from ventricular interdependence); dilated IVC + hepatic veins; hepatic vein Doppler: diastolic flow reversal on expiration; mitral inflow E velocity varies >25% with respiration. CARDIAC CATHETERISATION (gold standard): ''dip-and-plateau'' or ''square root sign'' on RV/LV pressure tracings (abrupt early diastolic filling then plateau); equalisation of all diastolic pressures (RA = RV diastolic = PCWP ± 5 mmHg); discordance of RV-LV systolic pressures with respiration (RV peak rises on inspiration, LV peak falls — ventricular interdependence). Treatment: PERICARDIECTOMY (surgical stripping) — curative; not urgent unless haemodynamic compromise."]','A','Equalisation of diastolic pressures on cath (RA = RV diastolic ≈ PCWP within 5 mmHg) is the hallmark of constrictive pericarditis — also seen in tamponade. Respirophasic discordance of ventricular pressures distinguishes constriction from restriction.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Pericardial Disease','hard','flashcard','How is tuberculous pericarditis managed, and what is the role of steroids?','["TB pericarditis management — highly relevant in Ghana/sub-Saharan Africa (TB most common cause of pericardial disease in these regions): DIAGNOSIS: pericardial fluid analysis — exudative (high protein, LDH), lymphocyte-predominant; ADA (adenosine deaminase) >40 U/L (highly sensitive marker for TB pericarditis); fluid AFB smear (low sensitivity ~40%); pericardial biopsy (diagnostic if fluid non-diagnostic — granulomas + AFB). TREATMENT: 1) STANDARD ANTI-TUBERCULOSIS THERAPY (ATT): 2HRZE / 4HR (rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol for 2 months; then rifampicin + isoniazid for 4 months — 6 months total). 2) CORTICOSTEROIDS: prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day for 4-8 weeks then taper — REDUCES constrictive pericarditis development (IMPI trial: steroids + ATT vs ATT alone; steroids reduced composite outcome including hospitalisation but did NOT reduce mortality or constriction in HIV-negative patients — controversial). 3) PERICARDIOCENTESIS: for haemodynamic compromise. 4) PERICARDIECTOMY: if constrictive pericarditis develops despite ATT + steroids."]','A','IMPI trial (2014): adjunctive steroids in TB pericarditis reduced hospitalisation and tamponade but NOT mortality or constrictive pericarditis in HIV-uninfected — current guidelines give corticosteroids a weak recommendation (may consider).','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','easy','flashcard','How is the ankle-brachial index (ABI) measured and how is it interpreted?','["Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI): doppler probe + BP cuff. Measure: brachial systolic BP (both arms -- use higher), then ankle systolic BP (dorsalis pedis AND posterior tibial -- use higher). ABI = ankle systolic / brachial systolic. INTERPRETATION: >1.3 = calcified, non-compressible vessels (DM/CKD/elderly) -- UNRELIABLE; use toe-brachial index or arterial duplex instead. 1.0-1.3 = NORMAL. 0.91-1.0 = borderline. 0.41-0.90 = mild-moderate PAD (claudication range); 0.41-0.69 = moderate; 0.70-0.90 = mild. <=0.40 = severe PAD / critical limb ischaemia (rest pain, tissue loss). After exercise: ABI falls significantly in PAD (unmasking disease) -- useful if resting ABI normal but claudication symptoms present. ABI should be measured in all patients with suspected PAD, DM with leg symptoms, or non-healing ulcers."]','A','ABI >1.3 is FALSE -- DO NOT trust it. In calcified vessels (DM, CKD), the artery cannot be compressed. Always use toe-brachial index (TBI <0.7 = PAD) or duplex in these patients.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the 6 Ps of acute limb ischaemia?','["The 6 Ps of acute limb ischaemia: 1) PAIN -- sudden severe limb pain (may be absent if complete motor/sensory loss). 2) PALLOR -- pale, white or mottled limb. 3) PULSELESSNESS -- absent distal pulses (Doppler detectable before clinical palpation fails). 4) PARAESTHESIA -- tingling/numbness (ischaemia of peripheral nerves -- early sign of neurological compromise). 5) PARALYSIS -- muscle weakness/inability to move (late sign -- indicates severe ischaemia; EMERGENCY). 6) PERISHING COLD -- cold limb compared to contralateral. Paraesthesia and paralysis indicate irreversible ischaemia if not treated within hours. Causes: embolism (AF, mural thrombus, valve disease) vs in-situ thrombosis (acute-on-chronic PAD). Emergency surgical or thrombolytic revascularisation required within 6 hours."]','A','PARALYSIS = irreversible ischaemia beginning -- neurological signs (paraesthesia then paralysis) mandate emergency revascularisation within hours to prevent amputation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','easy','flashcard','What is the Fontaine classification of peripheral arterial disease?','["Fontaine Classification of PAD: Stage I -- Asymptomatic; PAD detected only by ABI or imaging. Stage IIa -- Mild claudication; walking distance >200 metres. Stage IIb -- Moderate-severe claudication; walking distance <200 metres. Stage III -- Rest pain; ischaemic pain at rest (especially at night; hanging foot below heart relieves). Stage IV -- Ulceration or gangrene; tissue loss or dry/wet gangrene. Stages III-IV = CRITICAL LIMB ISCHAEMIA (CLI) -- requires urgent revascularisation. CLI definition: rest pain >2 weeks requiring analgesia OR ankle pressure <50 mmHg OR toe pressure <30 mmHg."]','A','Fontaine III-IV = Critical Limb Ischaemia -- high amputation risk and high cardiovascular mortality (5-year mortality ~50%). Urgent vascular surgery referral is mandatory.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','medium','flashcard','How do you clinically differentiate vascular claudication from neurogenic pseudoclaudication?','["Vascular claudication (PAD): calf pain; onset with WALKING; relieves with REST (standing still) in 1-5 min; consistent walking distance triggers pain; cold limb, absent pulses, low ABI; cycling reproduces pain. Neurogenic pseudoclaudication (lumbar spinal stenosis): buttock, thigh, or bilateral leg pain/paraesthesia; onset with STANDING or walking; relieved by SITTING or leaning forward (spinal flexion); variable walking distance; can cycle without pain (spine flexed); normal pulses, normal ABI; associated with back pain. Bicycle test: vascular = pain on cycling; neurogenic = no pain on cycling. MRI spine confirms neurogenic cause."]','A','The KEY differentiator: vascular claudication stops with rest (standing OK); neurogenic pseudoclaudication requires sitting or spinal flexion for relief. Bicycle test separates them.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the medical management of stable peripheral arterial disease (claudication)?','["Medical management of PAD (claudication): 1) ANTIPLATELET: aspirin 75mg OD or clopidogrel 75mg OD (preferred -- CAPRIE trial). 2) STATIN: high-intensity atorvastatin 40-80mg -- reduces MACE and improves walking distance. 3) ACE INHIBITOR: ramipril reduces cardiovascular events in PAD (HOPE trial). 4) SUPERVISED EXERCISE: 30-60 min treadmill walking 3x/week to near-maximal claudication -- most effective non-invasive intervention (+100-150% walking distance). 5) SMOKING CESSATION -- most important modifiable risk factor. 6) Cilostazol 100mg BD: PDE-3 inhibitor; vasodilator + antiplatelet; improves claudication ~50%; CONTRAINDICATED in HF."]','A','Supervised exercise therapy is as effective as angioplasty for improving walking distance in claudication -- it is the FIRST intervention before revascularisation in Fontaine IIa/IIb.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','medium','flashcard','What is Buerger''s disease (thromboangiitis obliterans) and how does it differ from atherosclerotic PAD?','["Buerger''s disease: inflammatory, non-atherosclerotic, segmental thrombotic occlusion of small/medium arteries AND veins of extremities. Demographics: young patients (20-40 years), heavy smokers (ALWAYS). Distribution: tibial, peroneal, radial, ulnar arteries (DISTAL vessels -- not aorto-iliac); hands AND feet affected. Features: claudication of foot/arch, rest pain, ischaemic ulcers at fingertips/toes, migratory superficial thrombophlebitis (episodic painful red cords). Angiogram: corkscrew collaterals around occlusions; no atherosclerosis. Treatment: COMPLETE SMOKING CESSATION (only treatment -- continuing smoking leads to amputation). No effective medical alternative."]','A','Buerger''s disease = YOUNG SMOKER with distal (foot/hand) ischaemia, migratory phlebitis, no atherosclerosis. Complete smoking cessation is the ONLY treatment.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the revascularisation options for critical limb ischaemia and when is amputation indicated?','["Critical limb ischaemia revascularisation: ENDOVASCULAR: balloon angioplasty +/- stenting -- preferred first approach for iliac/femoral lesions (TASC A/B). Drug-eluting balloons for infrapopliteal disease. SURGICAL: bypass grafting -- femoro-popliteal bypass using great saphenous vein (best conduit, 70% patency at 5yr), femoro-distal bypass for CLI. HYBRID procedures combine both approaches. AMPUTATION indications: irreversible ischaemia (muscle rigor, fixed paralysis), extensive tissue necrosis not amenable to reconstruction, severe systemic sepsis from infected gangrene, failed revascularisation. Level: most distal functional level with adequate perfusion -- trans-metatarsal > below-knee > above-knee."]','A','Saphenous vein is the BEST bypass conduit -- autologous vein has far superior long-term patency vs prosthetic PTFE graft. Preserve the saphenous vein from unnecessary stripping.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','hard','flashcard','A 65-year-old diabetic presents with a painless plantar ulcer on the 1st metatarsal head. How does this differ from an ischaemic ulcer and what is the management?','["Neuropathic vs ischaemic ulcer: NEUROPATHIC: plantar pressure points (metatarsal heads, heel); PAINLESS (peripheral neuropathy); surrounding callus; punched-out edges; warm foot; pulses PRESENT; ABI may be falsely high (calcified vessels in DM). ISCHAEMIC: tips of toes, lateral foot; PAINFUL; no callus; irregular/necrotic edges; cold foot; absent pulses; low ABI. This patient = NEUROPATHIC. Management: 1) OFFLOADING -- total contact cast or post-op shoe. 2) WOUND CARE -- debridement of callus and necrotic tissue. 3) INFECTION: swab for culture; antibiotics for cellulitis (clindamycin or co-amoxiclav mild; IV meropenem for severe). 4) GLYCAEMIC CONTROL -- HbA1c <53 mmol/mol. 5) Osteomyelitis screen: probe-to-bone test + MRI. 6) Vascular assessment even if pulses present."]','A','Probing to bone in a diabetic foot ulcer: positive probe-to-bone test has 89% PPV for osteomyelitis -- get an MRI to confirm and determine extent.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','hard','flashcard','How do you distinguish embolism from thrombosis in acute limb ischaemia, and what is the management?','["Acute limb ischaemia -- embolism vs thrombosis: EMBOLISM: sudden onset (minutes), no prior claudication, contralateral normal limb, identifiable source (AF, MI, valvular disease), angiogram shows sharp cut-off with normal proximal vessels. THROMBOSIS (acute-on-chronic): slower onset, prior claudication history, bilateral reduced ABIs, diffuse atherosclerosis on angiogram. Management by viability: 1) VIABLE (no neuro deficit): IV heparin 5000U bolus + infusion (prevents propagation) while planning revascularisation. 2) THREATENED (sensory loss +/- motor deficit): EMERGENCY revascularisation -- EMBOLISM: surgical embolectomy (Fogarty balloon catheter); THROMBOSIS: catheter-directed thrombolysis (CDT) with alteplase. 3) IRREVERSIBLE (paralysis + rigor): primary amputation. Post-procedure: treat source (anticoagulate AF)."]','A','The 6-hour rule: irreversible damage begins at 4-6h of complete ischaemia. Paraesthesia onset is the clinical clock-start for urgent revascularisation.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Peripheral Vascular Disease','hard','flashcard','What is the mechanism of cilostazol, its clinical benefit in PAD, and why is it contraindicated in heart failure?','["Cilostazol mechanism: selective phosphodiesterase-3 (PDE-3) inhibitor -- increases intracellular cAMP in platelets and vascular smooth muscle -- inhibits platelet aggregation + causes peripheral vasodilation. Clinical benefit in PAD: improves maximal walking distance by ~50-67% vs placebo; superior to pentoxifylline; class I indication for symptomatic claudication (ACC/AHA). Dose: 100mg BD on empty stomach. HF CONTRAINDICATION: PDE-3 inhibitors increase cardiac cAMP -- positive inotrope/chronotrope -- increased arrhythmias and mortality in HF (class effect, analogous to milrinone in PROMISE trial). Cilostazol is ABSOLUTELY CONTRAINDICATED in ANY degree of heart failure (any NYHA class). Also contraindicated: active bleeding, pregnancy."]','A','PDE-3 inhibitors in HF = increased arrhythmia and death (PROMISE trial with milrinone). Cilostazol shares this class contraindication -- never prescribe in any patient with heart failure.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','easy','flashcard','What is the Wells DVT score and how is it used clinically?','["Wells DVT Score -- pre-test probability for DVT: Active cancer (1pt), Paralysis/plaster cast (1pt), Bedridden >3 days or major surgery <12 weeks (1pt), Localised tenderness along deep venous system (1pt), Entire leg swollen (1pt), Calf swelling >3cm asymmetry (1pt), Pitting oedema confined to symptomatic leg (1pt), Dilated superficial veins (non-varicose) (1pt), Previous DVT (1pt), Alternative diagnosis at LEAST as likely (-2pts). Score interpretation: <=0 = Low probability (DVT unlikely) -- if D-dimer negative, DVT excluded. 1-2 = Moderate probability -- D-dimer if negative excludes; if positive, proceed to compression ultrasound. >=3 = High probability (DVT likely) -- proceed directly to compression ultrasound. D-dimer is only useful to RULE OUT DVT in low/moderate probability -- never use alone to confirm DVT."]','A','D-dimer has high sensitivity (>95%) but very low specificity -- it is used to RULE OUT, never to RULE IN. Elevated D-dimer has many causes: infection, malignancy, pregnancy, post-surgery, PE, DIC.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','easy','flashcard','What is the Ghana STG first-line treatment for DVT and what is the target INR?','["Ghana STG DVT treatment: 1st LINE: Unfractionated heparin (UFH) IV -- loading dose 80 units/kg IV bolus, then 18 units/kg/hour infusion; adjust to maintain aPTT 60-100 seconds (1.5-2.5x normal); start warfarin simultaneously. 2nd LINE: Enoxaparin (LMWH) SC 1.5mg/kg OD or 1mg/kg BD -- no monitoring required (use UFH if CrCl <30 mL/min). Warfarin: start day 1-2 alongside heparin; target INR 2.0-3.0; continue heparin until INR therapeutic for >=2 consecutive days. Duration of warfarin: provoked DVT (surgery, immobilisation) -- 3 months; unprovoked DVT -- 6 months; recurrent DVT or persistent risk factors (cancer, thrombophilia) -- indefinite anticoagulation. LMWH preferred over warfarin for cancer-associated DVT (less drug interactions, more predictable). Monitor INR weekly initially, then monthly when stable."]','A','''Bridge'' rule: continue heparin until INR >=2.0 for at least 2 consecutive days before stopping heparin -- warfarin has a paradoxical pro-thrombotic effect initially (protein C/S reduction before factor II/X reduction).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','easy','flashcard','What are the ECG findings in massive pulmonary embolism?','["ECG in PE: most common finding is SINUS TACHYCARDIA (non-specific but present in >40%). Classic pattern: S1Q3T3 -- deep S wave in lead I, Q wave and T wave inversion in lead III (right heart strain pattern). Other PE ECG signs: new RBBB (complete or incomplete) -- right ventricular pressure overload; T wave inversions in V1-V4 (right ventricular strain pattern); right axis deviation; P pulmonale (tall peaked P in lead II); atrial fibrillation or flutter (may precipitate). Normal ECG does NOT exclude PE. Most sensitive ECG finding: sinus tachycardia + T wave inversions in V1-V4. S1Q3T3 is CLASSIC but NOT sensitive (occurs in only ~20% of PE cases) -- its absence does not exclude PE. ECG + clinical context + D-dimer/CTPA confirms diagnosis."]','A','S1Q3T3 is the CLASSIC ECG pattern for PE but has only ~20% sensitivity -- sinus tachycardia + T inversions V1-V4 are far more common. Normal ECG does not exclude PE.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','medium','flashcard','How do you differentiate massive from submassive PE and what is the treatment for each?','["PE severity classification: MASSIVE PE: haemodynamic instability (SBP <90 mmHg for >15 min or requiring vasopressors, or cardiac arrest from PE). RV dysfunction + haemodynamic compromise. Treatment: SYSTEMIC THROMBOLYSIS -- alteplase 100mg IV over 2h (fibrinolytic); indicated if no absolute contraindications; reduces mortality but increases major bleeding (including ICH ~2%). If thrombolysis contraindicated or fails: catheter-directed thrombolysis or surgical embolectomy. SUBMASSIVE PE: normal BP but evidence of RV strain (RV dilation on echo or CT, elevated troponin or BNP) without haemodynamic compromise. Treatment: anticoagulation (LMWH or UFH); consider catheter-directed thrombolysis if high-risk submassive (rising troponin, worsening haemodynamics). LOW-RISK PE: normal BP, no RV strain, low PESI score -- anticoagulation alone; can consider outpatient management if PESI class I-II."]','A','Thrombolysis in PE is ONLY indicated for MASSIVE (haemodynamically unstable) PE -- submassive PE is treated with anticoagulation; catheter-directed lysis is increasingly used for submassive PE at risk of deterioration.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','medium','flashcard','What is the Wells PE score and when should CTPA vs V/Q scan be used?','["Wells PE Score: DVT clinical signs and symptoms (3pts), alternative diagnosis less likely than PE (3pts), HR >100 bpm (1.5pts), immobilisation >3 days or surgery in prior 4 weeks (1.5pts), prior DVT or PE (1.5pts), haemoptysis (1pt), malignancy (1pt). Score: <=4 = PE unlikely -- check D-dimer first; >4 = PE likely -- proceed directly to CTPA. CTPA (CT pulmonary angiography) vs V/Q scan: CTPA: preferred -- faster, widely available, allows alternative diagnosis (pneumonia, dissection); sensitivity 83-90%, specificity 96%; disadvantage: radiation, contrast nephropathy, CI in severe CKD. V/Q scan: preferred in -- renal impairment, contrast allergy, young women (breast radiation), pregnancy (lower fetal radiation than CTPA). Indeterminate V/Q requires CTPA or clinical follow-up. D-dimer: use to exclude PE in low/moderate pre-test probability only (high NPV); NOT useful in high probability or post-surgery/pregnancy (D-dimer always high)."]','A','In PREGNANCY suspected PE: V/Q is preferred over CTPA (lower maternal breast radiation dose); if V/Q indeterminate, CTPA is used. Bilateral compression ultrasound first -- if DVT confirmed, treat and avoid CT radiation.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','medium','flashcard','What is phlegmasia cerulea dolens and how is it managed?','["Phlegmasia cerulea dolens (PCD): severe, limb-threatening form of extensive DVT (complete occlusion of ileofemoral venous system including collaterals). Clinical features: MASSIVE oedema, CYANOSIS (dark blue/purple discolouration from venous congestion), intense PAIN, potential compartment syndrome, venous gangrene (arterial flow compromised by massive venous hypertension). Precursor: phlegmasia alba dolens (less severe -- white leg with DVT, no cyanosis). PCD is often associated with underlying malignancy (paraneoplastic hypercoagulability). Management: 1) Urgent anticoagulation -- IV heparin. 2) Limb elevation + monitoring for compartment syndrome (fasciotomy if needed). 3) CATHETER-DIRECTED THROMBOLYSIS (CDT) via popliteal or femoral vein catheter -- reduces thrombus burden, restores venous drainage. 4) Pharmacomechanical thrombectomy if CDT insufficient. 5) Surgical venous thrombectomy (last resort). Mortality from underlying cause (usually malignancy) is high."]','A','PCD is a DVT emergency -- cyanosis + massive oedema means near-complete venous occlusion. CDT (catheter-directed thrombolysis) is the intervention of choice for limb salvage.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','medium','flashcard','What are the indications for an IVC filter in VTE management?','["IVC (inferior vena cava) filter indications: ABSOLUTE (anticoagulation contraindicated or failed): 1) Proximal DVT or PE with absolute contraindication to anticoagulation (active intracranial haemorrhage, recent neurosurgery, uncontrolled major bleeding). 2) Recurrent PE despite adequate anticoagulation (anticoagulation failure). 3) Massive PE with haemodynamic instability -- as adjunct to thrombolysis/anticoagulation to prevent further emboli. RELATIVE: 4) Free-floating proximal DVT (controversial). 5) Prophylaxis in high-risk patients who cannot receive anticoagulation (major trauma, neurosurgery). RETRIEVABLE IVC filters: preferred over permanent -- retrieve at 2-4 weeks once anticoagulation can be safely restarted. Complications: filter thrombosis, DVT propagation, IVC occlusion, filter migration, penetration. IVC filters do NOT replace anticoagulation -- resume anticoagulation as soon as safely possible after filter insertion."]','A','IVC filters REDUCE PE but INCREASE DVT recurrence -- they are NOT a substitute for anticoagulation. Always plan to remove a retrievable filter and restart anticoagulation as soon as safe.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','hard','flashcard','How is the duration of anticoagulation in VTE determined and what factors favour indefinite therapy?','["VTE anticoagulation duration: PROVOKED DVT/PE (identifiable major transient risk factor: surgery, immobilisation, oestrogen): 3 months minimum -- low recurrence risk after stopping. UNPROVOKED DVT/PE (no identifiable cause): minimum 6 months; high recurrence risk after stopping (~30% at 5 years) -- assess bleeding risk and patient preference for indefinite therapy. INDEFINITE ANTICOAGULATION for: 1) Second unprovoked VTE. 2) Active cancer (cancer-associated thrombosis -- LMWH preferred; switch to DOAC when stable). 3) Major inherited thrombophilia: antiphospholipid syndrome (warfarin, not DOAC -- DOAC inferior in APS), homozygous Factor V Leiden, antithrombin deficiency, protein C/S deficiency. 4) Recurrent thrombosis despite anticoagulation. Assess at 3 months with DASH score (D-dimer, Age, Sex, Hormonal therapy) to guide extension. Extended therapy options: full-dose DOAC (rivaroxaban, apixaban) or low-dose aspirin (inferior but option if anticoagulation refused)."]','A','Antiphospholipid syndrome requires WARFARIN (not DOAC) for VTE -- DOAC was inferior in the TRAPS trial. APS + VTE = lifelong warfarin, target INR 2-3 (or 3-4 for arterial thrombosis).','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','hard','flashcard','A 55-year-old woman has massive PE with SBP 78 mmHg. Outline the emergency management.','["Massive PE emergency management: 1) IMMEDIATE: High-flow O2 (target SpO2 >=94%); IV access x2; bloods (FBC, U&E, troponin, BNP, ABG, D-dimer, clotting); 12-lead ECG; portable echo (RV dilation, McConnell sign -- apical sparing of RV with free wall hypokinesis). 2) HAEMODYNAMIC SUPPORT: careful IV fluid (250-500mL crystalloid -- excessive fluid worsens RV distension and causes septal shift); if hypotension persists -- noradrenaline IV (vasopressor of choice in obstructive shock -- maintains systemic BP without worsening RV afterload as much as adrenaline). 3) CTPA if patient can be safely transported (within 5 min); if not, clinical + echo + history sufficient for diagnosis. 4) SYSTEMIC THROMBOLYSIS: alteplase 100mg IV over 2h (or 50mg bolus in cardiac arrest -- ACLS protocol). 5) ANTICOAGULATION: UFH 80 units/kg bolus before thrombolysis; hold UFH during infusion; restart 1h after thrombolysis completed. 6) If thrombolysis contraindicated or fails within 60 min: catheter embolectomy or surgical embolectomy. 7) Consider ECMO for refractory cardiogenic shock."]','A','McConnell sign on echo (RV free wall hypokinesis with sparing of apex) is >77% specific for massive PE -- it can confirm PE haemodynamically before CTPA when the patient is too unstable to transport.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Deep Vein Thrombosis & Pulmonary Embolism','hard','flashcard','What is chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) and how is it diagnosed and treated?','["CTEPH: failure of clot resolution after PE -- organised thrombus obstructs pulmonary vasculature permanently -- progressive pulmonary hypertension. Occurs in 3-5% of patients after acute PE. Clinical: progressive exertional dyspnoea (months to years after PE), right heart failure (elevated JVP, peripheral oedema, ascites), exercise limitation. Suspect in: unexplained dyspnoea after PE, or new pulmonary hypertension. Investigations: 1) Echo: RV enlargement, elevated RVSP (estimated PA pressure). 2) V/Q SCAN: remains best screening test for CTEPH -- mismatched perfusion defects (CT may underestimate chronic organised thrombus). 3) Right heart catheterisation (gold standard: mPAP >=25 mmHg at rest + PCWP <=15 mmHg -- confirms precapillary PH). 4) CT pulmonary angiography: shows web-like intraluminal filling defects. Treatment: 1) PULMONARY ENDARTERECTOMY (PEA) -- potentially curative for surgically accessible disease. 2) RIOCIGUAT (soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator) -- for inoperable CTEPH (CHEST-1 trial). 3) Balloon pulmonary angioplasty for inoperable disease. 4) Lifelong anticoagulation."]','A','V/Q scan is the PREFERRED screening test for CTEPH (not CTPA) -- chronic organised thrombus appears as perfusion defects on V/Q but may be missed as wall thickening on CT, which looks for acute clot.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','easy','flashcard','Define shock and list the four main types with their haemodynamic profiles.','["Shock: life-threatening state of circulatory failure causing inadequate oxygen delivery (DO2) to meet cellular metabolic demand (VO2) -- tissue hypoperfusion and cellular hypoxia. Clinical markers: SBP <90 mmHg, HR >100, cold clammy extremities, altered consciousness, oliguria <0.5 mL/kg/h, raised lactate >2 mmol/L. Haemodynamic profiles (CO=cardiac output, SVR=systemic vascular resistance, PCWP=pulmonary capillary wedge pressure): HYPOVOLAEMIC: Low CO, High SVR, Low PCWP. CARDIOGENIC: Low CO, High SVR, High PCWP. DISTRIBUTIVE (septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic): High CO (early), Low SVR, Low PCWP. OBSTRUCTIVE (PE, tamponade, tension pneumothorax): Low CO, High SVR, High or Low PCWP (depends on cause). All types share low BP and end-organ hypoperfusion despite different underlying mechanisms."]','A','Mnemonic CHORD: Cardiogenic (High PCWP, Low CO, High SVR), Hypovolaemic (Low PCWP, Low CO, High SVR), Obstructive (variable), Redistributive/Distributive (Low SVR, High CO early). Use PCWP to distinguish cardiogenic from hypovolaemic.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','easy','flashcard','What are the causes of obstructive shock and how is each treated?','["Obstructive shock: mechanical impediment to cardiac output OUTSIDE the myocardium. Causes: 1) TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX: air accumulates under pressure in pleural space -- compresses mediastinum, kinks vena cava, reduces venous return. Signs: tracheal deviation AWAY from side, absent breath sounds, hypotension, JVP raised, hyperresonant to percussion. Treatment: IMMEDIATE needle decompression (2nd ICS midclavicular line) then chest drain. 2) CARDIAC TAMPONADE: pericardial fluid compresses heart. Signs: Beck''s triad (hypotension, elevated JVP, muffled heart sounds), pulsus paradoxus. Treatment: pericardiocentesis. 3) MASSIVE PE: right ventricular outflow obstruction. Treatment: thrombolysis, embolectomy. 4) AORTIC DISSECTION (type A): can cause tamponade or coronary occlusion. Treatment: emergency surgery. Tension pneumothorax is the most immediately lethal -- treat without waiting for imaging if suspected.']','A','TENSION pneumothorax = NEVER wait for CXR -- the diagnosis is CLINICAL (absent breath sounds, deviated trachea, hypotension). Needle decompression first, CXR after.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','easy','flashcard','What is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock and what is the initial fluid resuscitation volume?','["Septic shock management: FLUIDS first: IV crystalloid (normal saline or Hartmann''s/Ringer''s lactate) 30 mL/kg over 3 hours (Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021) -- reassess after each 500 mL bolus (fluid responsiveness: passive leg raise test increases CO >10% = fluid responsive). VASOPRESSOR: NORADRENALINE (norepinephrine) -- first-line vasopressor; alpha-1 agonist (vasoconstriction raises SVR) + mild beta-1 (maintains cardiac contractility); target MAP >=65 mmHg. Start if MAP <65 despite adequate fluid resuscitation. Second-line vasopressors: vasopressin 0.03-0.04 units/min (adds to noradrenaline effect, reduces noradrenaline dose -- VASST trial); adrenaline (if noradrenaline insufficient). AVOID: dopamine as vasopressor in septic shock (higher mortality than noradrenaline in SOAP-II trial, except in highly selected patients with bradycardia/low cardiac risk). ANTIBIOTICS: within 1 hour (broad-spectrum empiric then de-escalate based on cultures)."]','A','Surviving Sepsis 1-hour bundle (Hour-1): 1) Measure lactate, 2) Blood cultures, 3) Broad-spectrum antibiotics, 4) Crystalloid 30mL/kg, 5) Vasopressors if MAP <65. All within 1 hour of septic shock recognition.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','medium','flashcard','How is anaphylactic shock diagnosed and what is the treatment including dosing of adrenaline?','["Anaphylaxis: severe, life-threatening systemic hypersensitivity reaction. Diagnosis: rapid onset (minutes) after allergen exposure + >=1 of: skin/mucosa features (urticaria, angioedema, flushing) + respiratory compromise + hypotension or end-organ dysfunction. No skin features in ~20% (beware). TREATMENT -- ''SAVE A LIFE'' mnemonic: 1) ADRENALINE (epinephrine) 0.5mg IM into outer thigh (1:1000 solution = 1mg/mL -- give 0.5 mL); repeat every 5-10 min if no improvement. IM is first-line (NOT IV unless cardiac arrest or refractory). 2) CALL for help (999/emergency team). 3) POSITION: supine + legs elevated (or recovery position if unconscious). 4) HIGH-FLOW OXYGEN. 5) IV CRYSTALLOID 500-1000 mL rapidly. 6) ANTIHISTAMINE: chlorphenamine 10mg IM/IV (2nd line). 7) CORTICOSTEROID: hydrocortisone 200mg IV (prevents biphasic reaction). MONITOR: observe 6-24h (biphasic reaction in ~20% at 1-72h)."]','A','Adrenaline in anaphylaxis: 0.5mg IM (1:1000) -- NOT 0.5mg IV (ten times higher risk of dysrhythmia). IV adrenaline reserved for cardiac arrest or anaphylaxis refractory to multiple IM doses under cardiac monitoring.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','medium','flashcard','What are the clinical signs of neurogenic shock and how does it differ from hypovolaemic shock?','["Neurogenic shock: distributive shock from loss of sympathetic vasomotor tone after spinal cord injury (usually above T6). Mechanism: loss of sympathetic outflow below lesion -- widespread vasodilation (low SVR), absence of compensatory tachycardia. Clinical: HYPOTENSION + BRADYCARDIA (paradoxical -- unlike other shock types which cause tachycardia); warm, well-perfused skin (vasodilated, not vasoconstricted); absent sweating below injury level; priapism (sign of spinal cord injury). Haemodynamics: Low CO, Low SVR, Low PCWP, Low HR. Hypovolaemic shock: tachycardia (compensatory), cold clammy skin (vasoconstriction), oliguria, high SVR. MANAGEMENT of neurogenic shock: 1) Cautious IV fluids (do not over-resuscitate -- may worsen spinal cord oedema). 2) Vasopressors: noradrenaline or phenylephrine (alpha-agonists to increase SVR). 3) Atropine or pacing for bradycardia. 4) Spinal immobilisation + corticosteroid (methylprednisolone within 8h -- controversial). 5) Maintain MAP >=85-90 mmHg for spinal cord perfusion."]','A','Neurogenic shock = HYPOTENSION + BRADYCARDIA (warm) in spinal cord injury -- the BRADYCARDIA distinguishes it from all other shock types, which produce tachycardia as a compensatory response.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','medium','flashcard','What are the key components of the Surviving Sepsis Campaign bundle and the definition of septic shock?','["Sepsis definitions (Sepsis-3, 2016): Sepsis = life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by dysregulated host response to infection; SOFA score rise >=2 points from baseline (or qSOFA >=2 in non-ICU: RR >=22, altered mental status, SBP <=100). Septic shock = subset of sepsis with: persistent hypotension requiring vasopressors to maintain MAP >=65 mmHg + serum lactate >=2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation (mortality >40%). SIRS criteria are no longer used for sepsis definition. Surviving Sepsis Bundle (1-HOUR bundle): 1) Measure lactate (repeat if initial >2); 2) Blood cultures x2 before antibiotics; 3) Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour; 4) Crystalloid 30 mL/kg for hypotension or lactate >=4 mmol/L; 5) Vasopressors (noradrenaline) if MAP <65 despite fluids. Hydrocortisone 200mg/day: add to vasopressors if vasopressor-refractory septic shock. Glucose target: <10 mmol/L (avoid hypoglycaemia)."]','A','Lactate is the KEY biomarker in sepsis -- lactate >=4 mmol/L = high mortality, mandates aggressive resuscitation even without hypotension. Lactate clearance (>10% fall in 2h) is the resuscitation target.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','medium','flashcard','How do you use passive leg raising (PLR) to assess fluid responsiveness in shock?','["Passive leg raise (PLR) test: bedside, reversible method to predict fluid responsiveness WITHOUT giving fluid (useful in patients at risk of fluid overload, e.g., HF, ARDS). Technique: 1) Patient supine, HOB 45 degrees (semi-recumbent). 2) Measure CO/SV/BP at baseline. 3) Lower HOB to flat and raise legs to 45 degrees -- transfers 300-400 mL of venous blood from legs/splanchnic to central circulation (auto-transfusion). 4) Measure CO/SV/BP again after 1-2 minutes. 5) THEN RETURN PATIENT TO ORIGINAL POSITION (effect is fully reversible). Interpretation: CO/SV increase >=10-15% = FLUID RESPONSIVE (will benefit from IV fluids). <10% increase = NOT fluid responsive (do not give more fluid -- use vasopressors). Must measure CO dynamically (echocardiography, pulse pressure variation, PICCO, or non-invasive CO monitors -- not just BP, as BP can rise from vasoconstriction without improved CO). PLR predicts fluid responsiveness better than CVP or static filling pressures."]','A','CVP does NOT predict fluid responsiveness -- it is an unreliable measure of preload adequacy. Dynamic tests (PLR, pulse pressure variation, stroke volume variation) are superior and should replace CVP-guided resuscitation.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','hard','flashcard','Describe the haemodynamic profiles distinguishing cardiogenic from distributive shock and the specific management of each.','["Haemodynamic comparison using PA catheter (Swan-Ganz): CARDIOGENIC SHOCK: CO low (<2.2 L/min/m2), PCWP high (>18 mmHg -- congested), SVR high (compensatory vasoconstriction); cold, clammy, cyanosed, pulmonary crackles. Cause: MI, myocarditis, arrhythmia, valvular emergency. Treatment: inotrope (dobutamine), vasopressor if profound hypotension (noradrenaline), mechanical support (IABP, Impella), treat cause (PCI for MI). DISTRIBUTIVE (SEPTIC) SHOCK: CO high (>4.0 L/min/m2 early -- hyperdynamic), PCWP low-normal, SVR very low; warm, vasodilated, bounding pulses, fever. Treatment: fluids + noradrenaline + antibiotics + source control. MIXED SHOCK (e.g., sepsis + HF): high CO but inadequate for demand; complex -- needs haemodynamic monitoring to guide treatment. Bedside echo: rapidly distinguishes hyperdynamic (distributive) from hypokinetic (cardiogenic) LV -- guides therapy without PA catheter."]','A','Bedside POCUS (point-of-care ultrasound) has largely replaced PA catheter for shock differentiation -- IVC collapsibility, LV function, B-lines (pulmonary oedema), and pericardial effusion are assessed in <5 minutes.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','hard','flashcard','What is the pathophysiology of distributive shock in sepsis, and why does multi-organ failure develop?','["Septic shock pathophysiology: TRIGGER: bacterial endotoxin (LPS from gram-negative) or exotoxin (gram-positive) -- activates monocytes/macrophages via TLR4 -- massive cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6, IL-8). CARDIOVASCULAR: 1) Vasodilation: NO (nitric oxide) overproduction by iNOS -- profound arterial and venous dilation (low SVR). 2) Myocardial depression: TNF-alpha + IL-1 suppress cardiac contractility (despite high CO initially). 3) Maldistribution: microvascular shunting -- blood bypasses capillary beds, cells not receiving oxygen despite apparently adequate CO. CELLULAR: mitochondrial dysfunction (cytopathic hypoxia) -- cells cannot use oxygen even when delivered. MULTI-ORGAN FAILURE: endothelial activation -- coagulopathy (DIC), capillary leak (ARDS, oedema), acute tubular necrosis (AKI), hepatic failure. LATE PHASE: immunosuppression (lymphocyte apoptosis, monocyte exhaustion) -- secondary infections. This explains why lactate can be elevated even with normal CO -- O2 extraction is impaired at cellular level."]','A','Cytopathic hypoxia in sepsis: mitochondria cannot use O2 even when delivered adequately -- elevated lactate despite normal-high CO is the footprint. ScvO2 >70% with high lactate suggests mitochondrial dysfunction, not flow deficit.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed',
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Clinicals','Shock','hard','flashcard','A 40-year-old is hypotensive after a bee sting with urticaria and bronchospasm. Despite 3 doses of IM adrenaline and 2L saline, BP remains 60/40. What is the next management step?','["Refractory anaphylactic shock management: 1) IV ADRENALINE infusion: if refractory to multiple IM doses -- adrenaline 1mg in 100mL saline (10 mcg/mL); start at 0.5-1 mcg/min, titrate to BP. Must be on cardiac monitor (arrhythmia risk). 2) IV VASOPRESSORS: noradrenaline infusion for vasoplegic component (low SVR). 3) Methylene blue 1.5-2 mg/kg IV (inhibits iNOS -- reduces NO-mediated vasodilation in refractory anaphylaxis with vasoplegia). 4) IV GLUCAGON 1-2 mg bolus (useful if patient on beta-blockers -- glucagon bypasses beta-receptors, increases cAMP directly). 5) IV HYDROCORTISONE 200mg + IV CHLORPHENAMINE 10mg (antihistamine). 6) BRONCHOSPASM: salbutamol nebulised + IV magnesium sulphate 2g if severe. 7) Consider ECMO if cardiac arrest refractory to ACLS. 8) Identify and remove/neutralise allergen. ICU admission mandatory. Biphasic reaction monitoring x24h. Prescribe adrenaline auto-injector (EpiPen) on discharge + allergy referral."]','A','GLUCAGON is the rescue drug in anaphylaxis in patients on beta-blockers -- beta-blockers block adrenaline''s effect; glucagon activates adenylyl cyclase directly without needing beta-receptors.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed';